3}-{x}^{2}+1$ at $x=0.$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186120383 1074 "The signed curvature $k(x)$ of a plane curve $y=y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(x)=\\frac{{y}^{″}(x)}{(1+[{y}^{′}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${y}^{′}(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=6{x}^{2}-2x,\\,{y}^{″}(x)=12x-2\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the signed curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(x) & =\\frac{12x-2}{{[1+{(6{x}^{2}-2x)}^{2}]}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(0) & =\\frac{12(0)-2}{{[1+{(6\\cdot {0}^{2}-2\\cdot 0)}^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{{1}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186120387 1068 #edb/ref 17592186118406 13194139533420 17592186045585 false] [17592186120387 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320649 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186120387 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186120387 1071 "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t = π$ for the curve $r(t) = ⟨e^{\\sin t}, e^{\\cos t}⟩$." 13194139533420 17592186045585 false] [17592186120387 1071 "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=\\pi $ for the curve $r(t)=⟨{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t},\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}⟩.$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186120387 1074 "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(\\pi ),$ and $‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\pi ) & =⟨{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ,\\,-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-{e}^{0},\\,-{e}^{-1}\\cdot 0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖ & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+0}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{cos}^{2}⁡t,\\,-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{sin}^{2}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(\\pi ) & =⟨-{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\pi ,\\,-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0+1,{e}^{-1}+0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\frac{1}{e}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(\\pi )$ and ${r}^{″}(\\pi )$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi ) & =⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\times ⟨1,\\,\\frac{1}{e},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1/e & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1/e\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{e}\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi )‖ & =\\frac{1}{e}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature at $t=\\pi $ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\pi ) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi ){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi )‖}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{e})}=e.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186120392 1068 #edb/ref 17592186118436 13194139533420 17592186045585 false] [17592186120392 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320594 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186120392 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 