 [17592186320715 1079 #edb/ref 17592186319897 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320715 1080 #uuid "fee9d754-1e3f-5127-b952-ef485c0de56c" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320716 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320442 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320716 1069 "q-350534" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320716 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320716 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{\\sqrt{({x}^{4}+2)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320716 1072 #uuid "f4fafe02-cb7d-53b6-a541-a5c4876489aa" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320716 1074 "First, we substitute $u={x}^{4}+2.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4{x}^{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=4{x}^{3}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{4}+2$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{\\sqrt{({x}^{4}+2)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{\\sqrt{({x}^{4}+2)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }{u}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}{u}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-2{u}^{-1/2}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{u}^{-1/2}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{{a}^{1/2}}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{1/2}}] \\\\  & =-2\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320717 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320569 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320717 1069 "q-350537" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320717 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320717 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{10}{{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320717 1072 #uuid "5a6b3e30-3fec-5d6f-b78c-1aea76522c56" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320717 1074 "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{10}{{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x & =10{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}{x}^{-6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{-5}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-1)^{5}}-\\frac{1}{{a}^{5}}] \\\\  & =-2\\cdot (-1-0) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320718 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320322 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320718 1069 "q-350540" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320718 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320718 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{6}{(2x+1)^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320718 1072 #uuid "45df630e-f651-5e97-a712-bb9ab8cb5834" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320718 1074 "First, we substitute $u=2x+1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=2\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=2x+1$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $-\\infty $ | $-1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\infty $ | $-1$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{6}{(2x+1)^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{3}{{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{3}{{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u & =3{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}{u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{-3}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-1)^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{a}^{3}}] \\\\  & =-(-1-0) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320719 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320579 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320719 1069 "q-350541" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320719 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320719 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{8}{(3y-2)^{6}}\\,\\text{d}y=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320719 1072 #uuid "831e21bd-cf6a-5441-a556-4d450485c3a7" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320719 1074 "First, we substitute $u=3y-2.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}y}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=3\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $y\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=3y-2$ is as follows:\n\n| $y$ | $-\\infty $ | $0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\infty $ | $-2$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{8}{(3y-2)^{6}}\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{8}{3{u}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{8}{3{u}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{8}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-2}{u}^{-6}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{5}{u}^{-5}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{15}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{u}^{5}}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{15}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-2)^{5}}-\\frac{1}{{a}^{5}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{15}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{32}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{60}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320720 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320172 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320720 1069 "q-350542" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320720 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320720 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{10}{(4t+3)^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320720 1072 #uuid "f50581dc-047f-51aa-9c85-54d3cfe920fb" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320720 1074 "First, we substitute $u=4t+3.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=4\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=4\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $t\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=4t+3$ is as follows:\n\n| $t$ | $-\\infty $ | $-1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\infty $ | $-1$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{10}{(4t+3)^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{5}{2{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{5}{2{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{5}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}{u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{-3}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{6}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{6}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-1)^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{a}^{3}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{6}\\cdot (-1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320721 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320689 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320721 1069 "q-350543" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320721 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320721 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{12}{(5\\theta -1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320721 1072 #uuid "45f693e4-1a0c-5b3d-a5e5-3d444d6b2192" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320721 1074 "First, we substitute $u=5\\theta -1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}\\theta }=5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=5\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $\\theta \\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=5\\theta -1$ is as follows:\n\n| $\\theta $ | $-\\infty $ | $0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\infty $ | $-1$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{12}{(5\\theta -1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{12}{5{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{12}{5{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{12}{5}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{5}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-{u}^{-1}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{12}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\fr