"Given that $I(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4x+{t}^{5})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320724 1072 #uuid "29c2a1f5-1f49-561b-8327-5f1dab73915c" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320724 1074 "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${I}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nI(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4x+{t}^{5})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(4x+{t}^{5})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(4x+{t}^{5})^{2}] & =2(4x+{t}^{5})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(4x+{t}^{5}) \\\\  & =2(4x+{t}^{5})\\cdot 5{t}^{4} \\\\  & =10{t}^{4}(4x+{t}^{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}10{t}^{4}(4x+{t}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10{t}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4x+{t}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4x+{t}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}{|}_{0}^{1}+{t}^{5}x{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2+{t}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)=10{t}^{4}(2+{t}^{5}).\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320725 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320190 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320725 1069 "q-350549" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [1759218