^{3}/8}{log}_{3}⁡(8x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{1/8}^{{e}^{3}/8}{log}_{3}⁡(8x)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{3}⁡(8x)\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)} \\\\ {v}^{′}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int 1\\cdot \\,dx=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/8}^{{e}^{3}/8}{log}_{3}⁡(8x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1/8}^{{e}^{3}/8}u{v}^{′}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv{|}_{1/8}^{{e}^{3}/8}-{\\int }_{1/8}^{{e}^{3}/8}v{u}^{′}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡(8x)\\,(x){|}_{1/8}^{{e}^{3}/8}-{\\int }_{1/8}^{{e}^{3}/8}x(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[{log}_{3}⁡(8\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{3}}{8})\\,(\\frac{{e}^{3}}{8})-{log}_{3}⁡(8\\cdot \\frac{1}{8})\\,(\\frac{1}{8})]-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}{\\int }_{1/8}^{{e}^{3}/8}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{8}{log}_{3}⁡({e}^{3})-\\frac{1}{8}{log}_{3}⁡(1)-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}x{|}_{1/8}^{{e}^{3}/8} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{8}\\cdot \\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}(\\frac{{e}^{3}}{8}-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}(2{e}^{3}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320780 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320491 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320780 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320576 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320780 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320683 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320780 1069 "q-351021" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320780 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320780 1071 "Express $\\frac{2{x}^{2}+11x+7}{(x-1)(x+3)(x+4)}$ as a sum of partial fractions.\n\n| $\\frac{2{x}^{2}+11x+7}{(x-1)(x+3)(x+4)}=\\,$ | {{field-1}} | $+$ | {{field-2}} | $-$ | {{field-3}} |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $x-1$ |  | $x+3$ |  | $x+4$ |" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320780 1072 #uuid "a5af4fe4-5698-53ca-abee-a17c646de28d" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320780 1074 "Our task is to find $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\frac{2{x}^{2}+11x+7}{(x-1)(x+3)(x+4)}=\\frac{A}{x-1}+\\frac{B}{x+3}+\\frac{C}{x+4}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $(x-1)(x+3)(x+4)$ gives\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}+11x+7=A(x+3)(x+4)+B(x-1)(x+4)+C(x-1)(x+3).\n$$\n\nFor $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(1)^{2}+11(1)+7 & =A(1+3)(1+4)+B(1-1)(1+4)+C(1-1)(1+3) \\\\ 20 & =20A \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=-3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-3)^{2}+11(-3)+7 & =A(-3+3)(-3+4)+B(-3-1)(-3+4)+C(-3-1)(-3+3) \\\\ -8 & =-4B \\\\ B & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=-4$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-4)^{2}+11(-4)+7 & =A(-4+3)(-4+4)+B(-4-1)(-4+4)+C(-4-1)(-4+3) \\\\ -5 & =5C \\\\ C & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2{x}^{2}+11x+7}{(x-1)(x+3)(x+4)}=\\frac{1}{x-1}+\\frac{2}{x+3}-\\frac{1}{x+4}.\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320781 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320275 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320781 1069 "q-351027" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320781 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320781 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in ${\\text{m /s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t),$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. Find the velocity $v(t)$ (in $\\text{m/s}$) of the particle at time $t$ given that the particle has velocity $v=10\\,\\text{m /s}$ when $t=\\pi \\,\\text{s}.$\n\n$v(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320781 1072 #uuid "4c94b0f7-35f8-56c0-97b9-422a8e41d91e" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320781 1074 "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(\\pi )=10.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+C \\\\ 10 & =-6(-1)+C \\\\ 10 & =6+C \\\\ C & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+4.$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320782 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320223 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320782 1069 "q-351049" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320782 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320782 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{({e}^{2}-1)/4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x+1)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320782 1072 #uuid "33a27144-255d-5b60-b9f6-96470ef99a2b" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320782 1074 "First, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{({e}^{2}-1)/4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x+1)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{({e}^{2}-1)/4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x+1)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x+1)\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{4}{4x+1} \\\\ {v}^{′}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int 1\\cdot \\,dx=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{({e}^{2}-1)/4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x+1)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{({e}^{2}-1)/4}u{v}^{′}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv{|}_{0}^{({e}^{2}-1)/4}-{\\int }_{0}^{({e}^{2}-1)/4}v{u}^{′}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x+1)\\,(x){|}_{0}^{({e}^{2}-1)/4}-{\\int }_{0}^{({e}^{2}-1)/4}x(\\frac{4}{4x+1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2})(\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{4})-{\\int }_{0}^{({e}^{2}-1)/4}\\frac{4x}{4x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{4}\\cdot 2-{\\int }_{0}^{({e}^{2}-1)/4}(1-\\frac{1}{4x+1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{2}-{[x-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x+1)]}_{0}^{({e}^{2}-1)/4} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{2}-(\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{2}-(\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({e}^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320783 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320213 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320783 1069 "q-351051" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320783 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320783 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in ${\\text{ft/s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=\\frac{6}{{t}^{2}}-3,$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. Find the velocity $v(t)$ (in $\\text{ft/s}$) of the particle at time $t$ given that the particle has velocity $v=1\\,\\text{ft/s}$ when $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n$v(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320783 1072 #uuid "965ef9da-ae1f-5715-87b8-738b13ff060d" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320783 1074 "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{6}{{t}^{2}}-3)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{6}{{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+\\int (-3)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{t}-3t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(2)=1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =-\\frac{6}{2}-3(2)+C \\\\ 1 & =-9+C \\\\ C & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=-\\frac{6}{t}-3t+10.$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320784 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320220 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320784 1069 "q-351053" 1319413953