9-e01a-5b73-9848-2f1334dcbdb6" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320785 1074 "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 5{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5{e}^{t}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & =5{e}^{0}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =5+C \\\\ C & =-\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=5{e}^{t}-\\frac{9}{2}.$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =5{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =5(2)-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{2}-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{2}\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $\\frac{11}{2}\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320786 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320619 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320786 1069 "q-351059" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320786 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320786 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{t+1}},$ where $a(t)$ is measured in ${\\text{ft/s}}^{2}$ and $t$ is measured in seconds. Find the speed of the particle at the moment $t=3\\,\\text{s}$ given that the particle has velocity $v=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{ft/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nThe speed at the moment $t=3\\,\\text{s}$ is\n{{field-1}} $\\,\\text{ft/s}.$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320786 1072 #uuid "2f53cc96-d0e0-5baa-926b-aa6c419284ff" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320786 1074 "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{t+1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{t+1}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & =6\\sqrt{0+1}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =6+C \\\\ C & =-\\frac{11}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=6\\sqrt{t+1}-\\frac{11}{2}.$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=3\\,\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(3) & =6\\sqrt{3+1}-\\frac{11}{2} \\\\  & =6(2)-\\frac{11}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{2}-\\frac{11}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{2}\\,\\text{ft/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $\\frac{13}{2}\\,\\text{ft/s}.$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320787 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320267 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320787 1069 "q-351060" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320787 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320787 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)=-\\frac{4}{(t+1)^{2}},$ where $a(t)$ is measured in ${\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ and $t$ is measured in seconds. Find the speed of the particle at the moment $t=1\\,\\text{s}$ given that the particle has velocity $v=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nThe speed at the moment $t=1\\,\\text{s}$ is\n{{field-1}} $\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320787 1072 #uuid "b19684a6-9018-5197-b55c-cb9619211d38" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320787 1074 "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int -\\frac{4}{(t+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{t+1}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{4}{0+1}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =4+C \\\\ C & =-\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{4}{t+1}-\\frac{7}{2}.$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=1\\,\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =\\frac{4}{1+1}-\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2}-\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2}-\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320788 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320166 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320788 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320302 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320788 1069 "q-351063" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320788 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320788 1071 "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨3{sec}^{2}⁡(t),\\,-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(t)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(t)⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨2,\\,1⟩$ when $t=0,$ calculate the components of the the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t.$\n\n$v(t)=⟨${{field-1}},{{field-2}}$⟩$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320788 1072 #uuid "2118c3a9-2f0f-5d7e-a2e7-69417636adb1" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320788 1074 "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨3{sec}^{2}⁡(t),\\,-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(t)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(t)⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 3{sec}^{2}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\\int -2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(t)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(t)+{C}_{1},-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(t)+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(0)=⟨2,1⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =⟨3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(0)+{C}_{1},-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(0)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,1⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},-2+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & ={C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{1} & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =-2+{C}_{2} \\\\ {C}_{2} & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(t)+2,\\,-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(t)+3⟩.\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320789 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320326 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320789 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320449 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320789 1069 "q-351064" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320789 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320789 1071 "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨3\\sqrt{t},\\,-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{t+1}}⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨2,\\,-1⟩$ when $t=0,$ calculate the components of the the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t.$\n\n$v(t)=⟨${{field-1}},{{field-2}}$⟩$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320789 1072 #uuid "39247e6f-0cff-5de7-8e5a-6e25ff6675af" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320789 1074 "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨3\\sqrt{t},\\,-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{t+1}}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 3\\sqrt{t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{t+1}}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t\\sqrt{t}+{C}_{1},-8\\sqrt{t+1}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(0)=⟨2,-1⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =⟨2\\cdot 0\\cdot \\sqrt{0}+{C}_{1},-8\\sqrt{0+1}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,-1⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},-8+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & ={C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{1} & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =-8+{C}_{2} \\\\ {C}_{2} & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨2t\\sqrt{t}+2,\\,-8\\sqrt{t+1}+7⟩.\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320790 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320310 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320790 1069 "q-351102" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320790 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320790 1071 "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\,3\\sqrt{t}⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨-1,\\,-14⟩$ when $t=1,$ calculate the time at which the particle is stationary. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe particle is stationary when $t=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320790 1072 #uuid "369199ce-b3eb-55f4-a285-df32c2541d75" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320790 1074 "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to compute the velocity of the particle.\n\nTo compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},3\\sqrt{t}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 3\\sqrt{t}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{1/2}+{C}_{1},\\,2{t}^{3/2}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(1)=⟨-1,\\,-14⟩.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨{1}^{1/2}+{C}_{1},\\,2\\cdot {1}^{3/2}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,\\,-14⟩ & =⟨1+{C}_{1},\\,2+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we must have ${C}_{1}=-2$ and ${C}_{2}=-16.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨{t}^{1/2}-2,\\,2{t}^{3/2}-16⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times at which the particle is stationary, we solve for the times at which the velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}^{1/2}-2,\\,2{t}^{3/2}-16⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{1/2}-2=0 & \\,⇒\\,{t}^{1/2}=2\\,⇒\\,t=4, \\\\ 2{t}^{3/2}-16=0 & \\,⇒\\,{t}^{3/2}=8\\,⇒\\,t=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution that satisfies both equations is $t=4.$ So, this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary." 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320791 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320300 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320791 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320563 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320791 1069 "q-351105" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320791 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320791 1071 "The velocity vector $v=⟨u,v⟩$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨-4t+8,\\,5{t}^{2}+1⟩.\n$$\n\nCalculate the components of the acceleration vector $a$ at time $t.$\n\n$a=⟨${{field-1}},{{field-2}}$⟩$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320791 1072 #uuid "92d044e8-1301-55c7-a0d8-3e9c6a3f5869" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320791 1074 "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-4t+8),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5{t}^{2}+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4,\\,10t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320792 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320265 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320792 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320467 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320792 1069 "q-351106" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320792 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320792 1071 "The velocity vector $v=⟨u,v⟩$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨9t-4,\\,2{t}^{3}+5⟩.\n$$\n\nCalculate the components of the acceleration vector $a$ at time $t.$\n\n$a=⟨${{field-1}},{{field-2}}$⟩$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320792 1072 #uuid "dcfd915a-8223-5bbf-8255-dee535f46e3f" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320792 1074 "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(9t-4),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}+5)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨9,\\,6{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320793 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320191 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320793 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320601 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320793 1069 "q-351111" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320793 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320793 1071 "The velocity vector $v=⟨u,v⟩$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨{e}^{3t},\\,{t}^{3}⟩.\n$$\n\nCalculate the components of the acceleration vector $a$ at time $t.$\n\n$a=⟨${{field-1}},{{field-2}}$⟩$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320793 1072 #uuid "7494cd75-d518-5fb0-94ed-9812aee8822d" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320793 1074 "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{3t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{e}^{3t},\\,3{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320794 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320413 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320794 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320608 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320794 1069 "q-351116" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320794 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320794 1071 "The velocity vector $v=⟨u,v⟩$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨{2}^{t},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+1)⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the components of the acceleration vector $a$ at time $t.$\n\n$a=⟨${{field-1}},{{field-2}}$⟩$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320794 1072 #uuid "cf009670-6a73-521f-8ae7-ee7e6ec43b8d" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320794 1074 "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({2}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+1))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,\\frac{2t}{{t}^{2}+1}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320795 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320250 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320795 1069 "q-351132" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320795 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320795 1071 "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨t(6t-4),\\,12{t}^{3}⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(20,30)$ when $t=2,$ calculate the components of the position vector of $P$ at time $t.$\n\n$r(t)=⟨${{field-1}}$,\\,3{t}^{4}-18\\,⟩$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320795 1072 #uuid "969bc7bd-5757-583a-9a35-55c5b5346d2b" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320795 1074 "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨t(6t-4),\\,12{t}^{3}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int t(6t-4)\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 12{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}+{C}_{1},\\,3{t}^{4}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(2)=⟨20,30⟩.$ Substituting $t=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2) & =⟨2(2)^{3}-2(2)^{2}+{C}_{1},\\,3(2)^{4}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨20,30⟩ & =⟨8+{C}_{1},48+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=12$ and ${C}_{2}=-18.$ Therefore, the position vector is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}+12,\\,3{t}^{4}-18⟩.\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320796 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320282 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320796 1069 "q-35114" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320796 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320796 1071 "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ for the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩.$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320796 1072 #uuid "f89d804c-5610-5e9d-94e2-86a0ad458afd" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320796 1074 "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t),$ ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-2⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and ${r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨0,\\,-2,\\,0⟩\\times ⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =-2\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖ & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the radius of the osculating circle at  $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖}=\\frac{{2}^{3}}{\\,2\\,}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320797 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320229 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320797 1069 "q-351145" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320797 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320797 1071 "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨4{e}^{-4t},\\,5{e}^{5t}⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(1,6)$ when $t=0,$ calculate the components of the position vector of $P$ at time $t.$\n\n$r(t)=⟨\\,-{e}^{-4t}+2\\,,$\n{{field-1}}$⟩$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320797 1072 #uuid "89400d8f-bc26-5f99-a270-98d2b4472bd9" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320797 1074 "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨4{e}^{-4t},\\,5{e}^{5t}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 4{e}^{-4t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 5{e}^{5t}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-{e}^{-4t}+{C}_{1},\\,{e}^{5t}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=⟨1,6⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨-{e}^{-4(0)}+{C}_{1},\\,{e}^{5(0)}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,6⟩ & =⟨-1+{C}_{1},\\,1+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=2$ and ${C}_{2}=5.$ Therefore, the position vector is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨-{e}^{-4t}+2,\\,{e}^{5t}+5⟩.\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320798 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320446 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320798 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320687 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320798 1069 "q-351151" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320798 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320798 1071 "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨\\frac{1}{2t},\\,\\frac{1}{3t}⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $r=⟨1,2⟩$ when $t=1,$ calculate the position of $P$ at time $t=e.$ Do not round the answers.\n\n$r(e)=⟨${{field-1}},{{field-2}}$⟩$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320798 1072 #uuid "8827853e-f8d0-5541-b770-2b6065709709" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320798 1074 "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨\\frac{1}{2t},\\,\\frac{1}{3t}⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int \\frac{1}{2t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int \\frac{1}{3t}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+{C}_{1},\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(1)=⟨1,2⟩.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+{C}_{1},\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,2⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},\\,{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=1$ and ${C}_{2}=2.$ Therefore, the position vector at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+1,\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2⟩.\n$$\n\nTo find the position vector at time $t=e,$ we substitute $t=e$ into the above, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(e) & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)+1,\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)+2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}(1)+1,\\frac{1}{3}(1)+2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{7}{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320799 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320227 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320799 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320409 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320799 1069 "q-351156" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320799 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320799 1071 "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨6{t}^{2},\\,\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{t}⟩,\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $r=⟨-1,\\,2⟩$ when $t=0,$ calculate the position of $P$ at time $t=1.$ Do not round the answers.\n\n$r(1)=⟨${{field-1}},{{field-2}}$⟩$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320799 1072 #uuid "4593a68c-a815-5727-b2f3-870bf0137f29" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320799 1074 "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨6{t}^{2},\\,\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{t}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 6{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int \\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{t}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{t}^{3}+{C}_{1},\\,\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=⟨-1,2⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨2(0)^{3}+{C}_{1},\\,\\sqrt{(0)^{3}}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,2⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},\\,{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we must have ${C}_{1}=-1$ and ${C}_{2}=2.$ Therefore, the position vector at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨2{t}^{3}-1,\\,\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}+2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the position vector at time $t=1,$ we substitute $t=1$ into the above, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨2(1)^{3}-1,\\,\\sqrt{(1)^{3}}+2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2-1,\\,1+2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320800 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320308 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320800 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320711 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320800 1069 "q-351167" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320800 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320800 1071 "The velocity vector $v=⟨u,v⟩$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨4{t}^{3},\\,-2{t}^{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nCalculate the components of the acceleration vector $a$ at time $t=1.$ Do not round the answers.\n\n$a(1)=⟨${{field-1}},{{field-2}}$⟩$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320800 1072 #uuid "4776920e-6174-5578-87bc-008a6f01c217" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320800 1074 "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12{t}^{2},\\,-4t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the acceleration at time $t=1,$ we substitute $t=1$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(1) & =⟨12(1)^{2},\\,-4(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12,\\,-4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12,\\,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320801 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320599 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320801 1069 "q-351176" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320801 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320801 1071 "The velocity vector $v=⟨u,v⟩,$ measured in $\\text{m}/\\text{s},$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨3{t}^{2}+2t-1,-4t+5⟩.\n$$\n\nFind the magnitude of the acceleration of $P$ at time $t=1\\,\\text{s}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe magnitude of the acceleration is\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320801 1072 #uuid "7941bce9-a298-5f33-b1fd-29fc92dbaa1e" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320801 1074 "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}+2t-1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-4t+5)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6t+2,\\,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration at time $t=1,$ we substitute $t=1$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(1) & =⟨6(1)+2,-4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the magnitude of the acceleration, we compute $|a(1)|:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a(1)| & =|⟨8,-4⟩| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{8}^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{64+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{80} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnitude of the acceleration at time $t=1\\,\\text{s}$ is $4\\sqrt{5}{\\text{ m/s}}^{2}.$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320802 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320663 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320802 1069 "q-351180" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320802 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320802 1071 "Express $\\frac{2x+17}{(x+5)^{2}}$ as a sum of partial fractions.\n\n|  | $\\frac{2x+17}{(x+5)^{2}}\\,=\\,\\frac{2}{(x+5)}\\,\\,+$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $(x+5)^{2}$ |" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320802 1072 #uuid "7c9d6c9f-aca3-5a04-98fc-e0f6efe57d51" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320802 1074 "Our task is to find $B$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+17}{(x+5)^{2}}=\\frac{2}{(x+5)}+\\frac{B}{(x+5)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $(x+5)^{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+17 & =2(x+5)+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=-5$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-5)+17 & =2(-5+5)+B \\\\ -10+17 & =0+B \\\\ B & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{2x+17}{(x+5)^{2}}=\\frac{2}{(x+5)}+\\frac{7}{(x+5)^{2}}.\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320803 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320427 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320803 1069 "q-351184" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320803 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320803 1071 "Express $\\frac{3x+2}{(x-7)^{2}}$ as a sum of partial fractions.\n\n|  | $\\frac{3x+2}{(x-7)^{2}}\\,=\\,\\frac{3}{(x-7)}\\,\\,+$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $(x-7)^{2}$ |" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320803 1072 #uuid "51ebe86d-4fe0-5c86-8a6c-5ba06564711b" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320803 1074 "Our task is to find $B$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x+2}{(x-7)^{2}}=\\frac{3}{(x-7)}+\\frac{B}{(x-7)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $(x-7)^{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2 & =3(x-7)+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=7$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(7)+2 & =3(7-7)+B \\\\ 21+2 & =0+B \\\\ B & =23.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{3x+2}{(x-7)^{2}}=\\frac{3}{(x-7)}+\\frac{23}{(x-7)^{2}}.\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320804 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320203 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320804 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320635 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320804 1069 "q-351194" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320804 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320804 1071 "Express $\\frac{5x-6}{(x+1)^{2}}$ as a sum of partial fractions.\n\n| $\\frac{5x-6}{(x+1)^{2}}=$ | {{field-1}} | $-$ | {{field-2}} |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $x+1$ |  | $(x+1