+5)^{2}}.\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320806 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320274 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320806 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320343 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320806 1069 "q-351199" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320806 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320806 1071 "Express $\\frac{6x-11}{(x-4)^{2}}$ as a sum of partial fractions.\n\n| $\\frac{6x-11}{(x-4)^{2}}=$ | {{field-1}} | $+$ | {{field-2}} |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $x-4$ |  | $(x-4)^{2}$ |" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320806 1072 #uuid "b0c5b182-5082-54cb-9ad3-57eddc173df8" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320806 1074 "Our task is to find $A$ and $B$ such that\n\n$$\n\\frac{6x-11}{(x-4)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x-4}+\\frac{B}{(x-4)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x-4)^{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-11 & =A(x-4)+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=4$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(4)-11 & =A(4-4)+B \\\\ 13 & =B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=0$ and using our value for $B$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(0)-11 & =A(0-4)+13 \\\\ -11 & =-4A+13 \\\\ -4A & =-24 \\\\ A & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{6x-11}{(x-4)^{2}}=\\frac{6}{x-4}+\\frac{13}{(x-4)^{2}}.\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320807 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320402 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320807 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320497 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320807 1069 "q-351200" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320807 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320807 1071 "Express $\\frac{2x-9}{(x-3)^{2}}$ as a sum of partial fractions.\n\n| $\\frac{2x-9}{(x-3)^{2}}=$ | {{field-1}} | $-$ | {{field-2}} |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $x-3$ |  | $(x-3)^{2}$ |" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320807 1072 #uuid "ecf3a30b-2218-59fb-b78b-f76426b0a4d2" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320807 1074 "Our task is to find $A$ and $B$ such that\n\n$$\n\\frac{2x-9}{(x-3)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x-3}+\\frac{B}{(x-3)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x-3)^{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-9 & =A(x-3)+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=3$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(3)-9 & =A(3-3)+B \\\\ -3 & =B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=0$ and using our value for $B$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)-9 & =A(0-3)-3 \\\\ -9 & =-3A-3 \\\\ -3A & =-6 \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{2x-9}{(x-3)^{2}}=\\frac{2}{x-3}-\\frac{3}{(x-3)^{2}}.\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320808 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320231 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320808 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320547 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320808 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320600 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320808 1069 "q-351215" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320808 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320808 1071 "Express $\\frac{7{x}^{2}-18x+12}{x(x-2)^{2}}$ as a sum of partial fractions.\n\n| $\\frac{7{x}^{2}-18x+12}{x(x-2)^{2}}\\,=$ | {{field-1}} | $+$ | {{field-2}} | $+$ | {{field-3}} |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $x$ |  | $x-2$ |  | $(x-2)^{2}$ |" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320808 1072 #uuid "611997b0-b443-5169-9441-48b6053ff336" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320808 1074 "Our task is to find $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\frac{7{x}^{2}-18x+12}{x(x-2)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{B}{x-2}+\\frac{C}{(x-2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $x(x-2)^{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n7{x}^{2}-18x+12=A(x-2)^{2}+Bx(x-2)+Cx.\n$$\n\nLetting $x=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(0)^{2}-18(0)+12 & =A(0-2)^{2}+B(0)(0-2)+C(0) \\\\ 12 & =A(-2)^{2} \\\\ 12 & =4A \\\\ A & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(2)^{2}-18(2)+12 & =A(2-2)^{2}+B(2)(2-2)+C(2) \\\\ 4 & =C(2) \\\\ 4 & =2C \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, letting $x=1$ and using our values for $A$ and $C$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(1)^{2}-18(1)+12 & =3(1-2)^{2}+B(1)(1-2)+2(1) \\\\ 1 & =3(-1)^{2}+B(-1)+2 \\\\ 1 & =3-B+2 \\\\ 1 & =5-B \\\\ B & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{7{x}^{2}-18x+12}{x(x-2)^{2}}=\\frac{3}{x}+\\frac{4}{x-2}+\\frac{2}{(x-2)^{2}}.\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320809 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320207 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320809 1069 "q-35122" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320809 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320809 1071 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{5}+4).$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320809 1072 #uuid "57808d0b-eb9d-5a9c-ab3c-43a379853a6e" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320809 1074 "We identify the leading term and compute its limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{5}+4) & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{5}) \\\\  & =-\\infty \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320810 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320380 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320810 1069 "q-351223" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320810 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320810 1071 "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨8(1+2t)^{3},\\,4t⟩,\\,t\\in (0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $r=⟨1,\\,-2⟩$ when $t=0,$ find the times when the particle intersects the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe particle intersects the $x$-axis when $t=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320810 1072 #uuid "a6cb2379-e5d3-5a04-bd4c-c90a79eb692b" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320810 1074 "The particle intersects the $x$-axis when its position is $r=⟨x,0⟩,$ where $x$ is arbitrary. So first, we need to compute the position function of the particle.\n\nTo compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨8(1+2t)^{3},\\,4t⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 8(1+2t)^{3}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 4t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(1+2t)^{4}+{C}_{1},\\,2{t}^{2}+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=⟨1,-2⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨(1+0)^{4}+{C}_{1},\\,2(0)^{2}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,-2⟩ & =⟨1+{C}_{1},\\,{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=-2.$ Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(1+2t)^{4},\\,2{t}^{2}-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times when the particle intersects the $x$-axis, we solve for the times at which the $y$-coordinate of the position vector is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2}-2 & =0 \\\\ 2(t-1)(t+1) & =0 \\\\ t & =1,-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t$ must be positive, we conclude that the particle intersects the $x$-axis when $t=1.$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320811 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320545 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320811 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320610 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320811 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320669 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320811 1069 "q-351224" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320811 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320811 1071 "Express $\\frac{-18{x}^{2}-44x-38}{(x-1)(2x+3)^{2}}$ as a sum of partial fractions.\n\n| $\\frac{-18{x}^{2}-44x-38}{(x-1)(2x+3)^{2}}\\,=-$ | {{field-1}} | $-$ | {{field-2}} | $+$ | {{field-3}} |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $x-1$ |  | $2x+3$ |  | $(2x+3)^{2}$ |" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320811 1072 #uuid "20a54d13-99fe-556c-91a1-da478e12edce" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17