}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4x)]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4a).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4x)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4x)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/4d/4daa526482964d826d5cf17a9a646de0a01059e7f886ddd1e19bcb09c4c25ee3.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{1+16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4a) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320825 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320472 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320825 1069 "q-351320" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320825 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320825 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {4}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320825 1072 #uuid "5732541f-bf89-52a5-93a8-9630b62b9a53" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320825 1074 "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {4}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}3\\cdot {4}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{3\\cdot {4}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[3\\cdot {4}^{x}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(3-3\\cdot {4}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\cdot (3-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }3\\cdot {4}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={4}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{4}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{4}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/fd/fd4020291b348d130fc34969d72ff683aab5b6fb95ea5f7d32b90e5245d56d9c.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {4}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\cdot (3-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }3\\cdot {4}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\cdot (3-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320826 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320218 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320826 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320235 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320826 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320238 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320826 1069 "q-351326" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320826 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320826 1071 "Express $\\frac{-{x}^{2}+7x+1}{({x}^{2}+9)(x-2)}$ as a sum of partial fractions.\n\n| $\\frac{-{x}^{2}+7x+1}{({x}^{2}+9)(x-2)}=\\,$ | {{field-1}}$\\,x\\,+$\n{{field-2}} | $+$ | {{field-3}} |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $13({x}^{2}+9)$ |  | $13(x-2)$ |" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320826 1072 #uuid "8c2013d4-0b9c-5718-a377-8302917a7004" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320826 1074 "Our task is to find $A$, $B$, and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-{x}^{2}+7x+1}{({x}^{2}+9)(x-2)}=\\frac{Ax+B}{{x}^{2}+9}+\\frac{C}{x-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $({x}^{2}+9)(x-2)$ gives\n\n$$\n-{x}^{2}+7x+1=(Ax+B)(x-2)+C({x}^{2}+9).\n$$\n\nFor $x=2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(2)^{2}+7(2)+1 & =C(4+9) \\\\ 11 & =13C \\\\ C & =\\frac{11}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=0$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(0)^{2}+7(0)+1 & =(A(0)+B)(0-2)+\\frac{11}{13}((0)^{2}+9) \\\\ 1 & =-2B+\\frac{99}{13} \\\\ -2B & =-\\frac{86}{13} \\\\ B & =\\frac{43}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(1)^{2}+7(1)+1 & =(A(1)+\\frac{43}{13})(1-2)+\\frac{11}{13}((1)^{2}+9) \\\\ 7 & =-A-\\frac{43}{13}+\\frac{110}{13} \\\\ -A & =\\frac{24}{13} \\\\ A & =-\\frac{24}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-{x}^{2}+7x+1}{({x}^{2}+9)(x-2)} & =\\frac{(-\\frac{24}{13}x+\\frac{43}{13})}{{x}^{2}+9}+\\frac{(\\frac{11}{13})}{x-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-24\\,x+43}{13({x}^{2}+9)}+\\frac{11}{13(x-2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320827 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320348 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320827 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320495 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320827 1069 "q-351365" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320827 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320827 1071 "Find the range of values of $k$ for which the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{9}{{81}^{k}})}^{n}$\nconverges. Do not round the answer.\n\n$k$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320827 1072 #uuid "9c7cd9e5-fa91-50a5-a9a5-99520013d828" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320827 1074 "We see that this is a geometric series with a common ratio of $r=\\frac{9}{{81}^{k}}.$\n\nFor the series to converge, we require $|r|<1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{9}{{81}^{k}}| & <1 \\\\ \\frac{9}{{81}^{k}} & <1 \\\\ 9 & <{81}^{k} \\\\ {81}^{k} & >9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we could remove the absolute value bars because $\\frac{9}{{81}^{k}}$ is always positive.\n\nThe equation ${81}^{k}=9$ has the solution $k=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Now, since ${81}^{k}$ increases as $k$ increases, the solution to ${81}^{k}>9$ must be $k>\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320828 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320464 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320828 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320559 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320828 1069 "q-351372" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320828 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320828 1071 "Find the range of values of $k$ for which the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{6n}}{{9}^{kn}}$\nconverges. Do not round the answer.\n\n$k$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320828 1072 #uuid "0437df47-aa0c-5cbc-9d8e-1dd8f7c626fa" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320828 1074 "First, we rewrite the series so that it's in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }a{r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{6n}}{{9}^{kn}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{{3}^{6}}{{9}^{k}})}^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{{9}^{3}}{{9}^{k}})}^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{({9}^{3-k})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that this is a geometric series with a common ratio of $r={9}^{3-k}.$ \n\nFor the series to converge, we require $|r|<1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{9}^{3-k}| & <1 \\\\ {9}^{3-k} & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we could remove the absolute value bars because ${9}^{3-k}$ is always positive.\n\nThe equation ${9}^{3-k}=1$ has the solution $k=3.$ Now, since ${9}^{3-k}$ decreases as $k$ increases, the solution to ${9}^{3-k}<1$ must be $k>3.$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320829 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320522 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320829 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320556 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320829 1069 "q-351377" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320829 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320829 1071 "The position vector $r(t)$ of a particle $P$ relative to a fixed origin $O$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{t}^{3}-4{t}^{2},\\,2{t}^{4}-{t}^{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nCalculate the components of the acceleration vector $a$ at time $t.$\n\n$a=⟨${{field-1}},{{field-2}}$⟩$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320829 1072 #uuid "d87a0cb6-13ba-5ff7-bf4d-32cc8f3eca07" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320829 1074 "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector, which we can do by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-4{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{4}-{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2}-8t,\\,8{t}^{3}-2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}-8t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8{t}^{3}-2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6t-8,\\,24{t}^{2}-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320830 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320324 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320830 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320400 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320830 1069 "q-351381" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320830 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320830 1071 "The position vector $r(t)$ of a particle $P$ relative to a fixed origin $O$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨4{t}^{3}-2t,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t)⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the components of the acceleration vector $a$ at time $t.$\n\n$a=⟨${{field-1}},{{field-2}}$⟩$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320830 1072 #uuid "3787099f-7e74-5a8f-bb73-d3db26733f53" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320830 1074 "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector, which we can do by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{3}-2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12{t}^{2}-2,\\,\\frac{1}{t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(12{t}^{2}-2),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨24t,\\,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320831 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320379 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320831 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320548 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320831 1069 "q-351383" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320831 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320831 1071 "The position vector $r(t)$ of a particle $P$ relative to a fixed origin $O$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the components of the acceleration vector $a$ at time $t.$\n\n$a=⟨${{field-1}},{{field-2}}$⟩$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320831 1072 #uuid "a3ecc28c-ba89-55f3-b522-8221d5c66904" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320831 1074 "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector, which we can do by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)),\\,\\fra