\\\  & =⟨18-8,\\,12-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨10,\\,10⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the magnitude of the acceleration, we compute $|a(1)|:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a(1)| & =|⟨10,\\,10⟩| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{10}^{2}+{10}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{100+100} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{200} \\\\  & =10\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnitude of the acceleration at time $t=1\\,\\text{s}$ is $10\\sqrt{2}{\\text{ m/s}}^{2}.$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320837 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320386 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320837 1069 "q-351416" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320837 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320837 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{2}^{3x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320837 1072 #uuid "d12f1b97-ea9d-5c17-ae72-4951993938de" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320837 1074 "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {b}^{\\,px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p\\operatorname{ln}⁡(b)}{b}^{\\,px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{2}^{3x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1}{2}^{3x-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{2}^{3x}}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{2}^{3x}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(8-{2}^{3a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (8-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{3a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={2}^{3x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{2}^{3x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{2}^{3x}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/c2/c2af905a46cea7b7512a727a5e53a2a178e4902f7114f86439365684e6685efa.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{2}^{3x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (8-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{3a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (8-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320838 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320341 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320838 1069 "q-351417" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320838 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320838 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{5}^{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320838 1072 #uuid "bbbdee26-1dfc-589b-8462-825ef4d5dbf3" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320838 1074 "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {b}^{\\,px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p\\operatorname{ln}⁡(b)}{b}^{\\,px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{5}^{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{5}^{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{5\\cdot {5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{5}^{x}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(1-{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={5}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{5}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/2f/2fc15149d2097a1965e575e725b4dfb49401c1fe5bb1c5b3e43bf0f4f51743a1.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{5}^{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320839 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320256 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320839 1069 "q-351418" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320839 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320839 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{3}^{2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320839 1072 #uuid "5ddc4b62-1dd8-507f-9ebb-933c99d3bb1c" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320839 1074 "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {b}^{\\,px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p\\operatorname{ln}⁡(b)}{b}^{\\,px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{3}^{2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{3}^{2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{9\\cdot {3}^{2x}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{3}^{2x}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(1-{3}^{2a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{3}^{2a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={3}^{2x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{3}^{2x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{3}^{2x}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/05/052e461c4f02a644254b4b9936b688c29afacd07432db7234bd71474dc9a076c.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{3}^{2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{9}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{3}^{2a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320840 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320418 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320840 1069 "q-351420" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320840 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320840 1071 "A particle $Q$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t$ is given by $v(t)=-4t-1.$ Find the total displacement $d$ of the particle between $t=0$ and $t=1.$\n\n$d=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320840 1072 #uuid "e8d958cf-5bc4-5b78-ab71-5680efbe65d4" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320840 1074 "We find the total displacement by calculating the definite integral of the velocity.\n\nLet $d$ be the total displacement between $t=0$ and $t=1.$ Then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-4t-1)\\,\\text{dt} \\\\  & =(-2{t}^{2}-t){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[-2(1)^{2}-(1)]-[-2(0)^{2}-(0)] \\\\  & =[-2-1]-0 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320841 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320531 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320841 1069 "q-351421" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320841 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320841 1071 "A particle $Q$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t$ is given by $v(t)=6t+2.$ Find the total displacement $d$ of the particle between $t=1$ and $t=2.$\n\n$d=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320841 1072 #uuid "0b1adf90-c1f2-561b-8388-b75b15a07a78" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320841 1074 "We find the total displacement by calculating the definite integral of the velocity.\n\nLet $d$ be the total displacement between $t=1$ and $t=2.$ Then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(6t+2)\\,\\text{dt} \\\\  & =(3{t}^{2}+2t){|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[3(2)^{2}+2(2)]-[3(1)^{2}+2(1)] \\\\  & =[12+4]-[3+2] \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320842 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320318 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320842 1069 "q-351427" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320842 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320842 1071 "![](images/33/33939aec3ac1315771d36714d15b590980197aabf3c169cdea1a1108baf6d0fa.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{3}].\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320842 1072 #uuid "2f48ecf1-07a6-51f5-a6dc-b4e6133b86d6" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320842 1074 "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡t+9{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{9({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{9}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3t\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =3(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320843 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320272 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320843 1069 "q-351428" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320843 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320843 1071 "![](images/da/da2526ff7031c9dbc704b59e60c65df14dcd0678427e02c386fc16a86e1fc89c.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3,\\,y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1,\\,t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{5},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320843 1072 #uuid "4184edde-38d0-51b8-8041-b0992688a366" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320843 1074 "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3) \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1) \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /5}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /5}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{25{cos}^{2}⁡t+25{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /5}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{25({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /5}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{25}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{\\pi /5}^{\\pi /2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5t\\,{|}_{\\pi /5}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =5(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{5}) \\\\  & =5(\\frac{3\\pi }{10}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320844 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320370 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320844 1069 "q-351429" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320844 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320844 1071 "![](images/5f/5fdcb137c459d5631db3dc3d02ff33ca0b791619694f1badbe7f3f2d0b19141d.png)\n\nFind the arc length $L$ of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y=\\frac{1}{3}(2t+1)^{3/2},\\,t\\in [0,2].\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320844 1072 #uuid "97b78e76-2eb3-551c-ab96-c9852dc794ae" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320844 1074 "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{3}(2t+1)^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}(2t+1)^{1/2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =(2t+1)^{1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{(t)^{2}+{((2t+1)^{1/2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{(t+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}|t+1|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+t)\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{4}{2}+2)-0 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320845 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320468 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320845 1069 "q-351430" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320845 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320845 1071 "A particle $Q$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t$ is given by $v(t)=16-9{t}^{2}.$ Find the total displacement $d$ of the particle between $t=3$ and $t=4.$\n\n$d=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320845 1072 #uuid "a6f94c86-ad49-52aa-9af9-839dd69ec525" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320845 1074 "We find the total displacement by calculating the definite integral of the velocity.\n\nLet $d$ be the total displacement between $t=3$ and $t=4.$ Then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}(16-9{t}^{2})\\,\\text{dt} \\\\  & ={(16t-3{t}^{3})|}_{3}^{4} \\\\  & =[16(4)-3(4)^{3}]-[16(3)-3(3)^{3}] \\\\  & =[64-192]-[48-81] \\\\  & =-95.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320846 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320614 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320846 1069 "q-351431" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320846 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320846 1071 "A particle $Q$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t$ is given by $v(t)=\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{t-1}.$ Find the total displacement $d$ of the particle between $t=1$ and $t=5.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$d=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320846 1072 #uuid "accf1578-2829-54e7-9cd4-cbbd0e5affa9" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320846 1074 "We find the total displacement by calculating the definite integral of the velocity.\n\nLet $d$ be the total displacement between $t=1$ and $t=5.$ Then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={\\int }_{1}^{5}(\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{t-1})\\,\\text{dt} \\\\  & =(t-1)^{3/2}\\,{|}_{1}^{5} \\\\  & =(5-1)^{3/2}-(1-1)^{3/2} \\\\  & =8-0 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320847 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320551 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320847 1069 "q-351438" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320847 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320847 1071 "![](images/19/19f2adedfeac6e4c4af07c3d3742203020e10ce841dd22f8b2e22c36ee2bf8e1.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve $L$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx={e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y={e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$L=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}(${{field-1}}$)$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320847 1072 #uuid "212fc3da-e462-5e01-bc09-3c9becca9722" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320847 1074 "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{({e}^{2t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t))}^{2}+{({e}^{2t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t))}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{e}^{4t}{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{e}^{4t}{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{e}^{4t}({(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{e}^{4t}(4{cos}^{2}⁡t-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{sin}^{2}⁡t+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{e}^{4t}(4({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)+{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{5{e}^{4t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{5}\\,{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{2t}}{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}({e}^{\\pi }-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320848 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320358 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320848 1069 "q-351439" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320848 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320848 1071 "![](images/0b/0b9c4ebc430e05ef205e3d8108f2e1649bbc51b1a6b54e8f47b48e7ddad1e9a6.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve $L$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=4{cos}^{3}⁡t,\\,y=4{sin}^{3}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}].\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320848 1072 #uuid "2bc346f7-e655-5b4d-b5a3-0f310aa8fdb4" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320848 1074 "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{cos}^{3}⁡t) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3{cos}^{2}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{sin}^{3}⁡t) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =12{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{(-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t)}^{2}+{(12{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{144{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{4}⁡t+144{sin}^{4}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{144{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{144{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}12|\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}12\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320849 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320440 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320849 1069 "q-351442" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320849 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320849 1071 "A particle $Q$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t$ is given by $v(t)=\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}}.$ Find the total displacement $d$ of the particle between $t=0$ and $t=1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$d=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320849 1072 #uuid "4564b2a2-0a8e-5268-a3be-fbe43cc72dbd" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320849 1074 "We find the total displacement by calculating the definite integral of the velocity.\n\nLet $d$ be the total displacement between $t=0$ and $t=1.$ Then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}})\\,\\text{dt} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(t)){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320850 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320327 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320850 1069 "q-351444" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320850 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320850 1071 "A particle $Q$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t\\ge 0$ is given by $v(t)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(t)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(t).$ At time $t=0$ the particle is located at the position $x=3.$ \nFind the position of the particle at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe position of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is $x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320850 1072 #uuid "e3a63512-809e-5b05-9291-f33b8db86388" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320850 1074 "The position of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is equal to its position at $t=0$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{3}].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nCarrying out the computations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =3+{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(t)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3+{[\\operatorname{sec}⁡(t)]}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =3+\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(0) \\\\  & =3+2-1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the position of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is $x=4.$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320851 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320707 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320851 1069 "q-351445" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320851 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320851 1071 "A particle $Q$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t\\ge 0$ is given by $v(t)={sec}^{2}⁡(t).$ At time $t=0$ the particle is located at the position $x=-2.$ \nFind the position of the particle at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe position of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is $x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320851 1072 #uuid "e713da54-01ea-5836-a919-ed65d371d799" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320851 1074 "The position of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is equal to its position at $t=0$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nCarrying out the computations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =-2+{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{sec}^{2}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2+{[\\operatorname{tan}⁡(t)]}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-2+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(0) \\\\  & =-2+1-0 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the position of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is $x=-1.$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320852 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320198 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320852 1069 "q-351448" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320852 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320852 1071 "A particle $Q$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t\\ge 0$ is given by $v(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).$ At time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ the particle is located at the position $x=5.$ \nFind the position of the particle at time $t=\\pi .$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe position of the particle at $t=\\pi $ is $x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320852 1072 #uuid "aecd03ed-b7a5-5e40-a1b6-4cabde6a5915" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320852 1074 "The position of the particle at $t=\\pi $ is equal to its position at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(\\pi )=x(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nCarrying out the computations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(\\pi ) & =5+{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5+{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)]}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =5+[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )]-[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =5+(0-1)-(1+0) \\\\  & =5-2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the position of the particle at $t=\\pi $ is $x=3.$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320853 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320572 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320853 1069 "q-351449" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320853 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320853 1071 "A particle $Q$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t\\ge 0$ is given by $v(t)=6t-{t}^{2}.$ At time $t=3$ the particle is located at the position $x=2.$ \nFind the position of the particle at time $t=6.$\n\nThe position of the particle at $t=6$ is $x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320853 1072 #uuid "89b61747-d029-5a86-9d0b-a1477c756cfd" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320853 1074 "The position of the particle at $t=6$ is equal to its position at $t=3$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [3,6].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(6)=x(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{6}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nCarrying out the computations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(6) & =2+{\\int }_{3}^{6}(6t-{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2+{[3{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}]}_{3}^{6} \\\\  & =2+[3(6)^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}(6)^{3}]-[3(3)^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}(3)^{3}] \\\\  & =2+36-18 \\\\  & =2+18 \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the position of the particle at $t=6$ is $x=20.$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320854 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320703 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320854 1069 "q-351463" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320854 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320854 1071 "A particle $Q$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t\\ge 0$ is given by $v(t)={2}^{2t-1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2).$ At time $t=1$ the particle is located at the position $x=-5.$ \nFind the position of the particle at time $t=3.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe position of the particle at $t=3$ is $x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320854 1072 #uuid "a8f909fc-057d-5dae-9328-f52281df4c9f" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320854 1074 "The position of the particle at $t=3$ is equal to its position at $t=1$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [1,3].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(3)=x(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{3}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nCarrying out the computations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(3) & =-5+{\\int }_{1}^{3}({2}^{2t-1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-5+{[{2}^{2t-2}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =-5+{2}^{2(3)-2}-{2}^{2(1)-2} \\\\  & =-5+{2}^{4}-{2}^{0} \\\\  & =-5+16-1 \\\\  & =-5+15 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the position of the particle at $t=3$ is $x=10.$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320855 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320179 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320855 1069 "q-351471" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320855 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320855 1071 "![](images/a4/a4d2cf1820b792d804ab4d1b17ce1f36c0b63e4f406f8692d47f64a823b805f1.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320855 1072 #uuid "781cb0dd-c613-5bc2-b9c0-2614a05ddf91" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320855 1074 "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))}^{2}+{(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t-18\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+9{sin}^{2}⁡(3t)+9{cos}^{2}⁡t-18\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+9{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{9+9-18(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{18-18\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t-t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{18-18\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the double-angle formula for cosine, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)=1-2{sin}^{2}⁡t.$ Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡(t) \\\\ 1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =2{sin}^{2}⁡(t) \\\\ 18-18\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =36{sin}^{2}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{18-18\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{36{sin}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-6(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-6(-1-1) \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320856 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320217 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320856 1069 "q-351472" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320856 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320856 1071 "![](images/a3/a3fb5a3a6102485294de519cc1372b7a348235317336a1d4c7fdbcf88be019b2.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the velocity of an object moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis. At time $t=3,$ the particle is located at the position $x=3.$ What is the position of the particle at time $t=7?$\n\nThe position of the particle at $t=7$ is $x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320856 1072 #uuid "a8cbd0cf-c19d-5a80-81e5-f23ac7b8d4cb" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320856 1074 "The position of 