ed}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{((6t+1)^{1/2})}^{2}+(3t)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{6t+1+9{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{(3t+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}|3t+1|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{2}+t)\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{2}+1)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320858 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320314 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320858 1069 "q-351479" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320858 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320858 1071 "![](images/4c/4c50effa85cc82dfc22a65f3792780a99b2876b77de173993b43dead23b0c1d7.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the velocity of an object moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis. At time $t=0,$ the particle is located at the position $x=-4.$ What is the position of the particle at time $t=6?$\n\nThe position of the particle at $t=6$ is $x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320858 1072 #uuid "e5de4677-3b20-59f9-a79c-26e210f08f93" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320858 1074 "The position of the particle at $t=6$ is equal to its position at $t=0$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [0,6].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(6)=x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{6}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nWe are told that $x(0)=-4$, and we can compute the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{6}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ by finding the signed area under the graph from $t=0$ to $t=6.$ Computing the signed area, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{6}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t=10.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx(6)=-4+10=6.\n$$\n\nSo the position of the particle at $t=6$ is $x=6.$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320859 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320691 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320859 1069 "q-351487" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320859 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320859 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\sqrt[5]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320859 1072 #uuid "837f696d-d20a-5c36-a05d-18c283348d12" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320859 1074 "First, let's rewrite the integral using a substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe use the table below to change the limits:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nCarrying out the change of variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\sqrt[5]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[5]{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{-1/5}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration.\n\n$$\nf(u)={u}^{-1/5}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{ as }\\,u\\to {0}^{+}.\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{-1/5}\\,\\text{d}u & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{1}{u}^{-1/5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\frac{5}{4}{u}^{4/5}|}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[\\frac{5}{4}\\cdot {1}^{4/5}-\\frac{5}{4}\\cdot {a}^{4/5}] \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\sqrt[5]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320860 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320338 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320860 1069 "q-351500" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320860 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320860 1071 "![](images/42/4245a22d62605e68022fc2f02358f8f900e5370a02c04c973d0ea359249f62b6.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the velocity of an object moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis. At time $t=0,$ the particle is located at the position $x=4.$ What is the position of the particle at time $t=4?$\n\nThe position of the particle at $t=4$ is $x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320860 1072 #uuid "64aee68b-d125-5540-999d-15015f1c5489" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320860 1074 "The position of the particle at $t=4$ is equal to its position at $t=0$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [0,4].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(4)=x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{4}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nWe are told that $x(0)=4$, and we can compute the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{4}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ by finding the signed area under the graph from $t=0$ to $t=4.$ Computing the signed area, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{4}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx(4)=4+(-1)=3.\n$$\n\nSo the position of the particle at $t=4$ is $x=3.$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320861 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320452 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320861 1069 "q-351501" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320861 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320861 1071 "![](images/98/981252abc3668e62a5c9289c918d1231ec9a83745f70a00f0da4c85f194b8215.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the velocity of an object moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis. At time $t=4,$ the particle is located at the position $x=-3.$ What is the position of the particle at 