gned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{6}^{-2\\sqrt{x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{6}^{2a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320866 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320359 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320866 1069 "q-351513" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320866 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320866 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{3x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320866 1072 #uuid "54262066-df6e-5fe6-82dc-0066cbb9af54" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320866 1074 "First, we substitute $u=\\sqrt{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,⟹\\,2\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\sqrt{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{3x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{3x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}u}}{\\sqrt{3}}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{3}a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{3}x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}=0$\n\n![](images/1c/1c0fc31d392bbaccf9a292553321bd60ccbdb2a02f3675543797f570c4fa0ef1.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{3x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{3}a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320867 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320269 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320867 1069 "q-351517" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320867 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320867 1071 "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨48(2t+1)^{2},\\,-6⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(2,-1)$ and has velocity $⟨8,\\,5⟩$ when $t=0,$ calculate the position vector of $P$ at time $t.$\n\n$r(t)=⟨${{field-1}}$,\\,-3{t}^{2}+5t-1\\,⟩$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320867 1072 #uuid "91eeeee4-0ee2-5aa5-a2ed-737539174590" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320867 1074 "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector. To find the velocity vector, we first need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨48(2t+1)^{2},\\,-6⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 48(2t+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int (-6)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8(2t+1)^{3}+{C}_{1},\\,-6t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(0)=⟨8,5⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =⟨8(1)^{3}+{C}_{1},\\,-6(0)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨8,5⟩ & =⟨8+{C}_{1},\\,{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso, we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=5.$ Therefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t)=⟨8(2t+1)^{3},\\,-6t+5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate the velocity vector to obtain the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨8(2t+1)^{3},\\,-6t+5⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 8(2t+1)^{3}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int (-6t+5)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(2t+1)^{4}+{C}_{3},\\,-3{t}^{2}+5t+{C}_{4}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=(2,-1).$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨(1)^{4}+{C}_{3},\\,0+0+{C}_{4}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,-1⟩ & =⟨1+{C}_{3},\\,{C}_{4}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso, we must have ${C}_{3}=1$ and ${C}_{4}=-1.$ Therefore, the position vector of $P$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(2t+1)^{4}+1,\\,-3{t}^{2}+5t-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320868 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320533 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320868 1069 "q-351518" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320868 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320868 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320868 1072 #uuid "e61363af-f017-5544-b24c-982f536da975" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320868 1074 "First, we substitute $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{3}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{3}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{3}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{3}^{-u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{3}^{-u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{3}^{a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{3}^{x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{3}^{x}}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{3}^{x}}=0$\n\n![](images/fa/fac2eaeb6dfdda240d2bad7107f7a7e26ff688e89976437a95b99bc3f2ac288f.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{3}^{a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320869 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320183 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320869 1069 "q-351523" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320869 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320869 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\f