,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{4}^{a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320870 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320392 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320870 1069 "q-351528" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320870 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320870 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{3/x}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320870 1072 #uuid "b5ad2441-9b46-53b1-b926-e73815ccad85" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320870 1074 "First, we substitute $u=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,⟹\\,-\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to {0}^{-}$ and $u\\to 0$ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\frac{1}{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\infty $ | $0$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{3/x}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{0}^{-\\infty }{e}^{3u}\\,\\text{d}u={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{3u}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{3/x}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{e}^{3u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{e}^{3u}}{3}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(1-{e}^{3a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{3a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{3x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{3x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{3x}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/34/34abda46dc9fc2c6f14ce4f8460ba076a16004bb9141eb8074cdc08e0c12fd2f.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{3/x}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}(1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{3a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320871 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320658 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320871 1069 "q-351529" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320871 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320871 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{5/x}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320871 1072 #uuid "03e6a884-8ec8-5f16-aaa3-741331f42326" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320871 1074 "First, we substitute $u=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,⟹\\,-\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to {0}^{-}$ and $u\\to 0$ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\frac{1}{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\infty $ | $0$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{5/x}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{0}^{-\\infty }{e}^{5u}\\,\\text{d}u={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{5u}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{5/x}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{e}^{5u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{e}^{5u}}{5}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(1-{e}^{5a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{5a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{5x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{5x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{5x}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/2a/2a24352ca6421b3aa61f3f29e3c19373cca011feafce263775a2f692f67ad36b.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{5/x}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{5}(1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{5a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320872 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320528 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320872 1069 "q-351538" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320872 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320872 1071 "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨-{e}^{-t},\\,20{t}^{3}⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(3,-2)$ and has velocity $⟨0,\\,1⟩$ when $t=0,$ calculate the position vector of $P$ at time $t.$\n\n$r(t)=⟨${{field-1}}$,\\,{t}^{5}+t-2\\,⟩$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320872 1072 #uuid "f1162a9d-dcef-5e69-9118-446fc47020f2" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320872 1074 "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector. To find the velocity vector, we first need to integrate the acceleration vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨-{e}^{-t},\\,20{t}^{3}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int (-{e}^{-t})\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 20{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{-t}+{C}_{1},\\,5{t}^{4}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(0)=⟨0,1⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =⟨{e}^{0}+{C}_{1},\\,5(0)^{4}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,1⟩ & =⟨1+{C}_{1},\\,{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso, we must have ${C}_{1}=-1$ and ${C}_{2}=1.$ Therefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨{e}^{-t}-1,\\,5{t}^{4}+1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the position vector by integrating the velocity vector.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨{e}^{-t}-1,\\,5{t}^{4}+1⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int ({e}^{-t}-1)\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int (5{t}^{4}+1)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-{e}^{-t}-t+{C}_{3},\\,{t}^{5}+t+{C}_{4}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=⟨3,-2⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨-{e}^{0}-0+{C}_{3},\\,{0}^{5}+0+{C}_{4}⟩ \\\\ ⟨3,-2⟩ & =⟨-1+{C}_{3},\\,{C}_{4}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso, we must have ${C}_{3}=4$ and ${C}_{4}=-2.$ Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨-{e}^{-t}-t+4,\\,{t}^{5}+t-2⟩.\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320873 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320297 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320873 1069 "q-351539" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320873 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320873 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{3/x-1}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320873 1072 #uuid "bd9864a2-55c0-5632-8507-8351c7bead6d" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320873 1074 "First, we substitute $u=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,⟹\\,-\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to {0}^{-}$ and $u\\to 0$ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\frac{1}{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\infty $ | $0$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{3/x-1}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{0}^{-\\infty }{e}^{3u-1}\\,\\text{d}u={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{3u-1}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{3/x-1}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{e}^{3u-1}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{e}^{3u-1}}{3}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{-1}-{e}^{3a-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-1}}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{3a-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{3x-1}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{3x-1}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{3x-1}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/6a/6a5fd63a29d7c8f5fba712c7fa6c20620c9a1438750f3197606e726a85666827.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{3/x-1}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{e}^{-1}}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{3a-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-1}}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320874 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320514 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320874 1069 "q-351545" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320874 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320874 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320874 1072 #uuid "aaf1e23c-5c2a-568b-9798-e760d6ad39ea" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320874 1074 "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{2x}{3})|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{2a}{3})] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot [0-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320875 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320458 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320875 1069 "q-351551" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320875 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320875 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t)=8{t}^{3}+12,$ where $t>0$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=10$ when $t=1,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t.$\n\n$x(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320875 1072 #uuid "4bce2d79-7084-5a63-9869-78a39322f223" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320875 1074 "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (8{t}^{3}+12)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}+12t+C \\\\  & =2{t}^{4}+12t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $x(1)=10.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =2(1)^{4}+12(1)+C \\\\ 10 & =2+12+C \\\\ C & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=2{t}^{4}+12t-4.\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320876 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320404 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320876 1069 "q-351552" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320876 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320876 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t)=6{t}^{2}+8,$ where $t>0$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=15$ when $t=1,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t.$\n\n$x(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320876 1072 #uuid "e9146e33-9996-554f-84a8-151ce32ee8a1" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320876 1074 "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (6{t}^{2}+8)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}+8t+C \\\\  & =2{t}^{3}+8t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $x(1)=15.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15 & =2(1)^{3}+8(1)+C \\\\ 15 & =2+8+C \\\\ C & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=2{t}^{3}+8t+5.\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320877 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320634 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320877 1069 "q-351554" 1319413953342