s method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x+2=2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}+2=\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=\\frac{7}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{21}{8} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{21}{8}=\\frac{41}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{4}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{7}{2}$ | $\\frac{21}{8}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{41}{8}$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that  $y\\,(\\frac{3}{2})≈\\frac{41}{8}.$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320893 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320182 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320893 1069 "q-351665" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320893 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320893 1071 "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4x+4,\\,y(1)=0.\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(4).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$y(4)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320893 1072 #uuid "7d0dcfe7-9c5a-5a02-82f8-aae19f0583da" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320893 1074 "First, note that we are given $y(1)$ and asked to find $y(4).$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=4$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=0.$ \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x+4=4\\cdot 1+4=8 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=8\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}=12 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =0+Δy=0+12=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $8$ | $12$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $12$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x+4=4\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}+4=14 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=14\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}=21 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}=4 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=12+21=33\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $8$ | $12$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $12$ | $14$ | $21$ |\n| $4$ | $33$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that  $y(4)≈33.$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320894 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320374 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320894 1069 "q-351667" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320894 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320894 1071 "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{y},\\,y(0)=2.\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(1).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$y(1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320894 1072 #uuid "e91cd753-8370-58e0-baf3-48eb6706d9b1" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320894 1074 "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(1).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=1$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-0}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=2.$ \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{x}{y}=\\frac{0}{2}=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =2+Δy=2+0=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{x}{y}=\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{2}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+\\frac{1}{8}=\\frac{17}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $2$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{17}{8}$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that  $y(1)≈\\frac{17}{8}.$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320895 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320260 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320895 1069 "q-351668" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320895 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320895 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(y+x)^{2},\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the second row of the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{4}?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $$ | $$ | $$ | {{field-1}} |" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320895 1072 #uuid "37f8c555-7821-591a-8afb-93a7b0ae463c" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320895 1074 "To get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row), and we compute \n$Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(y+x)^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{5}{4}+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $\\frac{9}{4}$ | $\\frac{9}{16}$ |" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320896 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320211 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320896 1069 "q-351669" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320896 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320896 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3\\sqrt{xy},\\,y(4)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed 