\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320957 1072 #uuid "87fa74fe-6971-54c0-89c0-8e4080caab7c" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320957 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡4.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}-t)={e}^{t}-1, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{e}^{t/2})=2{e}^{t/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}4{e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{{({e}^{t}-1)}^{2}+{(2{e}^{t/2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}4{e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{t}+1+4{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}4{e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+2{e}^{t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}4{e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{({e}^{t}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}4{e}^{t/2}({e}^{t}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}8\\pi {e}^{t/2}({e}^{t}+1)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u={e}^{t/2}.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{t/2}\\,\\text{d}t,$ or $2\\,\\text{d}u={e}^{t/2}\\,\\text{d}t.$ We also update the limits of integration for $u:$\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}8\\pi ({e}^{t}+1)\\cdot {e}^{t/2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}8\\pi ({u}^{2}+1)\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =16\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({u}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =16\\pi {[\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}+u]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =16\\pi ((\\frac{8}{3}+2)-(\\frac{1}{3}+1)) \\\\  & =16\\pi (\\frac{14}{3}-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & =16\\pi (\\frac{10}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{160\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320958 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320257 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320958 1069 "q-352110" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320958 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320958 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+5){e}^{2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320958 1072 #uuid "b58bbd94-9f13-5f88-af03-80b43422d375" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320958 1074 "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={x}^{2}+5\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{2x+2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {e}^{2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{e}^{2x+2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({x}^{2}+5){e}^{2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+5)(\\frac{{e}^{2x+2}}{2})-\\int (\\frac{{e}^{2x+2}}{2})(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+5){e}^{2x+2}}{2}-\\int x\\,{e}^{2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int x\\,{e}^{2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{2x+2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {e}^{2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{e}^{2x+2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\,{e}^{2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(x)(\\frac{{e}^{2x+2}}{2})-\\int (\\frac{{e}^{2x+2}}{2})(1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x\\,{e}^{2x+2}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x\\,{e}^{2x+2}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2x+2}}{4}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({x}^{2}+5){e}^{2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+5){e}^{2x+2}}{2}-\\int x\\,{e}^{2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+5){e}^{2x+2}}{2}-(\\frac{x\\,{e}^{2x+2}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2x+2}}{4}+D) \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+5){e}^{2x+2}}{2}-\\frac{x\\,{e}^{2x+2}}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{2x+2}}{4}-D \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x+2}}{4}(2{x}^{2}-2x+11)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-D.$ \n\nFinally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+5){e}^{2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{{e}^{2x+2}}{4}(2{x}^{2}-2x+11)]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{e}^{2(1)+2}}{4}(2(1)^{2}-2(1)+11))-(\\frac{{e}^{2(0)+2}}{4}(2(0)^{2}-2(0)+11)) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{4}}{4}(2-2+11)-\\frac{{e}^{2}}{4}(0-0+11) \\\\  & =\\frac{11{e}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{11{e}^{2}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{11{e}^{4}-11{e}^{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320959 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320459 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320959 1069 "q-352111" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320959 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320959 1