n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{3}⁡(2x)\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {log}_{3}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({log}_{3}⁡(2x))(x)-\\int (x)(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{log}_{3}⁡(2x)-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{log}_{3}⁡(2x)-\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2({log}_{3}⁡(2x))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =2x{({log}_{3}⁡(2x))}^{2}-\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(x{log}_{3}⁡(2x)-\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+D) \\\\  & =2x{({log}_{3}⁡(2x))}^{2}-\\frac{4x{log}_{3}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+\\frac{4x}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2x{ln}^{2}⁡(2x)}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}-\\frac{4x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}+\\frac{4x}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(2{ln}^{2}⁡(2x)-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+4)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-\\frac{4D}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.$\n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula ${log}_{3}⁡(2x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.$\n\nFinally, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/2}^{e/2}2({log}_{3}⁡(2x))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{x}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(2{ln}^{2}⁡(2x)-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+4)]}_{1/2}^{e/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{e/2}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(2{ln}^{2}⁡e-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+4)-\\frac{1/2}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(2{ln}^{2}⁡1-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{e/2}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(2-4+4)-\\frac{1/2}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(0-0+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}-\\frac{2}{{ln}^{2}⁡3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(\\,e-2\\,)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 1319413