139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320987 1072 #uuid "dbb775dc-4bbf-583a-b653-f8d55cea39e1" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320987 1074 "To compute $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}},$ we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=7t+2,$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}({t}^{5}+1)=5{t}^{4}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(7t+2)}{5{t}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(7)}{5{t}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{5{t}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate at $t=2,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,2}=\\frac{7}{5({2}^{4})}=\\frac{7}{80}.\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320988 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320248 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320988 1069 "q-352392" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320988 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320988 1071 "A curve has parametric equations $x={e}^{2t},\\,y={e}^{6t}.$ Given that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3{e}^{4t},$ calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at the point where $t=1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,1}=$ \n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320988 1072 #uuid "d15a1b6f-6c04-5867-9ba2-82052fbbb3c4" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320988 1074 "To compute $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}},$ we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=3{e}^{4t},$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}({e}^{2t})=2{e}^{2t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(3{e}^{4t})}{2{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12{e}^{4t}}{2{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =6{e}^{2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate at $t=1,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,1}=6{e}^{2(1)}=6{e}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320989 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320695 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320989 1069 "q-352401" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320989 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320989 1071 "A curve has parametric equations $x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\,y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-1.$ Given that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3t),$ determine $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{9}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,\\pi /9}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320989 1072 #uuid "41b11065-6f41-5afd-b891-205d34604e79" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320989 1074 "To compute $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}},$ we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3t),$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3t))}{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-(3{sec}^{2}⁡(3t)))}{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{3}⁡(3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{9},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,\\pi /9}=-\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{3}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{9})=-4.\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320990 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320236 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320990 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320288 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320990 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320313 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320990 1069 "q-352424" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320990 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320990 1071 "$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{3t} \\\\ -10{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{3t} \\\\ 2{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the vector-valued functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ above. Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions of the same system of linear ODEs, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},\nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.  \n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320990 1072 #uuid "a7aaa65e-f08c-526d-97d3-4530bb3e25db" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320990 1074 "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, our Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{3t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ -10{e}^{3t} & 2{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(5{e}^{3t})\\cdot (2{e}^{3t})-(-{e}^{3t})\\cdot (-10{e}^{3t}) \\\\  & =10{e}^{6t}-10{e}^{6t} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{zero for all }t\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly $\\text{dependent}$ on $R.$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320991 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320246 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320991 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320515 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320991 1069 "q-352426" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320991 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320991 1071 "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{8x-7}{5x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}} $-\\,\\frac{43}{25}\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-2}} $)$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320991 1072 #uuid "93ee8d84-36a3-5326-b7c6-e94746ade2ac" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320991 1074 "First, we pull out the factor of $5$ from the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{8x-7}{5x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{8x-7}{5(x+\\frac{1}{5})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{8x-7}{(x+\\frac{1}{5})}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we draw up our synthetic division table to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder.\n\n|  | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $-\\frac{1}{5}$ | $8$ | $-7$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-\\frac{8}{5}$ |\n|  | $8$ | $-\\frac{43}{5}$ |\n\nSo, from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8x-7}{(x+\\frac{1}{5})} & =8-\\frac{(\\frac{43}{5})}{(x+\\frac{1}{5})} \\\\  & =8-\\frac{43}{5x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{8x-7}{5x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(8-\\frac{43}{5x+1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[8{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}x-43{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{5x+1}\\,\\text{d}x] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{[8x-\\frac{43}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5x+1|]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[8(1-0)-\\frac{43}{5}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|6|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1|)] \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}-\\frac{43}{25}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320992 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320367 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320992 1069 "q-35243" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320992 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320992 1071 "![](images/67/67b77d2d3798e87593c7dddeea93603fff36d9153f53248cc2dab295f4879321.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x).$ Find ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{f(x)}.$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320992 1072 #uuid "1ffa1d84-420d-51e5-b5d5-2102976566da" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320992 1074 "Applying the product rule and the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{f(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{f(x)}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot ({lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{f(x)}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{({lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x))}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=3.$ Therefore, the above becomes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{f(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{2}$ is an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $\\frac{1}{3}$ does not change the sign of the limit. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2})={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{2})=\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320993 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320432 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320993 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320455 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320993 1069 "q-352489" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320993 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320993 1071 "![](images/50/50ed8b3093adf431f41e6332fa43ac122df79c98d487961037443c77b4290fb7.png)\n\nFor the polar curve $r=6-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ shown above, calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ as a function of $\\theta .$ Fill in the missing parts in the result below.\n\n| $\\text{d}y$ | $=$ | {{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{d}x$ |  | $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,-$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ |" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320993 1072 #uuid "309c1ff0-040e-5815-998e-9055aeea8759" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320993 1074 "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=6-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(6-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =(6-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we used the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ).$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =6{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{′}-{({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =6(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{′} \\\\  & =6{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′}-\\frac{1}{2}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{′} \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320994 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320583 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320994 1069 "q-352491" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320994 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320994 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\pi /15}^{0}8\\operatorname{sec}⁡5x(\\operatorname{tan}⁡5x-{cos}^{2}⁡5x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320994 1072 #uuid "ff9f5fe1-b925-5646-a0bd-3096b5a14063" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320994 1074 "First, we multiply out the parentheses and then use the identity  $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /15}^{0}8\\operatorname{sec}⁡5x(\\operatorname{tan}⁡5x-{cos}^{2}⁡5x)\\,\\text{d}x & =8{\\int }_{-\\pi /15}^{0}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡5x\\operatorname{tan}⁡5x-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x}\\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{-\\pi /15}^{0}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡5x\\operatorname{tan}⁡5x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=5x.$ Then $\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u.$ \n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=5x.$\n\n| $x$ | $-\\frac{\\pi }{15}$ | $0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}8{\\int }_{-\\pi /15}^{0}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡5x\\operatorname{tan}⁡5x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x)\\,\\text{d}x & =8{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{0}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡u\\operatorname{tan}⁡u-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u)\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{0}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡u\\operatorname{tan}⁡u-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡u-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u){|}_{-\\pi /3}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡0-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0)-\\frac{8}{5}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{3})) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}(1-0)-\\frac{8}{5}(2+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{5}-\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320995 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320290 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320995 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320294 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320995 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320589 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320995 1069 "q-352512" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320995 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186320995 1071 "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=-2$ 