$C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}},$ where ${c}_{1}=5{b}_{2}$ and ${c}_{2}=-2{b}_{1}?$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321007 1072 #uuid "c8522320-65f1-531f-bee8-8f3ee0c26cf8" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321007 1074 "Since $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]$, we can write $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3{b}_{1}-6{b}_{2} \\\\  & =-6{b}_{2}+3{b}_{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{5}(5{b}_{2})-\\frac{3}{2}(-2{b}_{1}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{5}{c}_{1}-\\frac{3}{2}{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{6}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321008 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320403 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321008 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320688 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321008 1069 "q-352570" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321008 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321008 1071 "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨5\\sqrt{1+8t},\\,(3+2t)^{3/2}⟩,\\,0<t<4.\n$$\n\nCalculate the components of the velocity vector $v$ at time $t=3.$ Do not round the answers.\n\n$v(3)=⟨${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$⟩$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321008 1072 #uuid "d916d75a-94d3-5d7b-8418-919a918a6a64" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321008 1074 "The particle's velocity vector is given by the derivative of its position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\sqrt{1+8t}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((3+2t)^{3/2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(1+8t)^{-1/2}\\cdot 8,\\frac{3}{2}(3+2t)^{1/2}\\cdot 2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{20}{\\sqrt{1+8t}},3\\sqrt{3+2t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=3,$ we substitute into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(3) & =⟨\\frac{20}{\\sqrt{1+8(3)}},3\\sqrt{3+2(3)}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{20}{\\sqrt{25}},3\\sqrt{9}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{20}{5},3(3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,9⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321009 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320685 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321009 1069 "q-352572" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321009 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321009 1071 "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\frac{5{t}^{2}}{2},\\,3t+2⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed  $|v|$ of the particle at time $t=3.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe speed at time $t=3$ is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321009 1072 #uuid "d1725e15-5f76-5162-9ce2-16e2248edbdf" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321009 1074 "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{5{t}^{2}}{2}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t+2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5t,\\,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=3,$ we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(3) & =⟨5(3),\\,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨15,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed at $t=3$ we calculate the magnitude of $v(3),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|v(3)| & =\\sqrt{{15}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{225+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{234} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{26}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321010 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320611 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321010 1069 "q-352578" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321010 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321010 1071 "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2y+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $P(0,0)$ lies on the graph of the function, evaluate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at $P.$\n\n$\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,{|}_{(0,0)}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321010 1072 #uuid "0953702f-10a1-574d-afac-c897a1d024d1" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321010 1074 "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2y+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2y)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ into the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =2(2y+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =4y+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,{|}_{(0,0)} & =(4y+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,{|}_{(0,0)} \\\\  & =4(0)+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321011 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320342 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321011 1069 "q-352583" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321011 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321011 1071 "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\frac{{4}^{t-1}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4},\\frac{{9}^{t-1}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed of the particle at time $t=1.$\n\nThe speed at time $t=1$ is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321011 1072 #uuid "e63aed73-e499-59e7-bc58-279b9066439a" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321011 1074 "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{4}^{t-1}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{9}^{t-1}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡9})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{{4}^{t-1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4},\\frac{{9}^{t-1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{4}^{t-1},{9}^{t-1}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=1,$ we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨{4}^{1-1},{9}^{1-1}⟩=⟨1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed at $t=1$ we calculate the magnitude of $v(1),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|v(1)| & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321012 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320710 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321012 1069 "q-352584" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321012 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321012 1071 "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+2),\\,\\frac{4}{t}⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed $|v|$ of the particle at time $t=2.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe speed at time $t=2$ is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321012 1072 #uui