rname{cos}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321054 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320575 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321054 1069 "q-352868" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321054 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321054 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321054 1072 #uuid "6c92829c-a2f6-56f6-ab9b-8cc246269a34" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321054 1074 "We can rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}$ and $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡4x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=4x.$ Then $\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=4x.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ |\n\nTherefore, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}(cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{1}{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321055 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320631 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321055 1069 "q-35287" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321055 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321055 1071 "If \n$C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -6 & -9\\end{aligned}]$, then $\\text{rank}(C)=$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321055 1072 #uuid "3da673d4-649b-58dd-8fc3-59991161f657" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321055 1074 "To find the rank of $C,$ we first reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -6 & -9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows).\n\nTherefore, $\\text{rank}(C)=2.$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321056 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320626 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321056 1069 "q-352870" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321056 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321056 1071 "Compute the arc length $L$ of $y=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{8}+\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}}$ for $x\\in [1,2].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$ \n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321056 1072 #uuid "50520627-69a0-5672-92d6-61584ad60ba6" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321056 1074 "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{8}+\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}},$ $a=1,$ and $b=2.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}}]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{6}-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{4{x}^{6}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{6}+\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{4{x}^{6}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}}$ to the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{4}-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}})|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{8}(2)^{4}-\\frac{1}{4(2)^{2}}]-[\\frac{1}{8}(1)^{4}-\\frac{1}{4(1)^{2}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{31}{16}-(-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{33}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321057 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320637 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321057 1069 "q-352876" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321057 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321057 1071 "Find the equilibrium solution $y(x)$ of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y-7)^{2}.$\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321057 1072 #uuid "ec2bb2dd-3d57-56c5-a097-6d25e9c8032d" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321057 1074 "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(x)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nSo, to find the equilibrium solutions of a differential equation, we must find all values of $c$ such that $f(c)=0.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $f(y)=(y-7)^{2},$ so we attempt solve the equation $f(c)=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c) & =0 \\\\ (c-7)^{2} & =0 \\\\ c-7 & =0 \\\\ c & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y(x)=7$ is the only equilibrium solution of the given differential equation." 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321058 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320557 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321058 1069 "q-352879" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321058 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321058 1071 "Given that $I(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-{e}^{-t})^{3}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=3{e}^{-t}(\\,$\n{{field-1}}$)$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321058 1072 #uuid "83276408-d239-590a-9b6c-5fea141efbe3" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321058 1074 "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${I}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nI(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-{e}^{-t})^{3}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[({x}^{2}-{e}^{-t})^{3}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[({x}^{2}-{e}^{-t})^{3}] & =3({x}^{2}-{e}^{-t})^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}({x}^{2}-{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =3({x}^{2}-{e}^{-t})^{2}\\cdot {e}^{-t} \\\\  & =3{e}^{-t}({x}^{2}-{e}^{-t})^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{e}^{-t}({x}^{2}-{e}^{-t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{e}^{-t}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-{e}^{-t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-{e}^{-t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}{e}^{-t}+{e}^{-2t})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{1}2{x}^{2}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-2t}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}{|}_{0}^{1}-{e}^{-t}\\cdot \\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}{|}_{0}^{1}+{e}^{-2t}\\cdot x{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{-t}+{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)=3{e}^{-t}(\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{-t}+{e}^{-2t}).\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321059 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320206 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321059 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320567 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321059 1069 "q-352884" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321059 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321059 1071 "**In ascending order**, the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}={y}^{2}-2y-15$\nare\n\n${y}_{1}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}(t)=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321059 1072 #uuid "3bdd0c7e-41c4-51e1-82c0-ecf39c99be45" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321059 1074 "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(t)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nSo, to find the equilibrium solutions of a differential equation, we must find all values of $c$ such that $f(c)=0.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $f(y)={y}^{2}-2y-15,$ so we solve the equation $f(c)=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c) & =0 \\\\ {c}^{2}-2c-15 & =0 \\\\ (c-5)(c+3) & =0 \\\\ c & =-3,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, ${y}_{1}(t)=-3$ and ${y}_{2}(t)=5$ are the equilibrium solutions of the given differential equation." 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321060 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320376 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321060 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320513 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321060 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320646 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321060 1069 "q-352891" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321060 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321060 1071 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y=6{e}^{-5x}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321060 1072 #uuid "acd94b15-1ccc-5761-9f57-2219a962c911" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321060 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{-2x})}^{′}+2(u{e}^{-2x}) & =6{e}^{-5x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-2x}-2u{e}^{-2x}+2u{e}^{-2x} & =6{e}^{-5x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-2x}-\\cancel{2u{e}^{-2x}}+\\cancel{2u{e}^{-2x}} & =6{e}^{-5x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & =6{e}^{-5x} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =6{e}^{-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =6{e}^{-3x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 6{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =-2{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{-2x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{-2x} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-3x}\\cdot {e}^{-2x} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321061 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320204 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321061 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320216 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321061 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320356 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321061 1069 "q-352892" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321061 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321061 1071 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+5y=2{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321061 1072 #uuid "c31d8465-1742-5fcb-b49c-85e93bc3da30" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321061 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+5{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-5x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-5x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{-5x})}^{′}+5(u{e}^{-5x}) & =2{e}^{-x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-5x}-5u{e}^{-5x}+5u{e}^{-5x} & =2{e}^{-x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-5x}-\\cancel{5u{e}^{-5x}}+\\cancel{5u{e}^{-5x}} & =2{e}^{-x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-5x} & =2{e}^{-x} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =2{e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =2{e}^{4x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{-5x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{-5x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{4x}\\cdot {e}^{-5x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321062 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320698 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321062 1069 "q-352909" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321062 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321062 1071 "![](images/92/921338bafb4c56c560502d2ec526703031b0261a5f9c71fcdd0d44058920787e.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(t)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be $f(t),$ where $u$ denotes the unit step function?" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321062 1072 #uuid "c20ad491-f32a-5dbf-b879-248572f5db4f" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321062 1074 "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nu(t)={\\begin{aligned}1,\\,t\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\,t<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=u(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/f5/f5b06b3481472b5be7f4d71da1df3192962c383059a1e7fb36f98426058738ae.png)\n\nNotice that to transform $y=u(t)$ to the given function $y=f(t),$ we perform the following transformations:\n\n- First, we reflect $y=u(t)$ in the $y$-axis, which gives $y=u(-t).$\n\n- Next, we shift $y=u(-t)$ vertically upwards by $2$ units, giving $y=u(-t)+2.$\n\n- There is no horizontal shift or vertical scaling.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(t)=u(-t)+2.$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321063 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320457 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321063 1069 "q-352913" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321063 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321063 1071 "![](images/fe/fe876b6075fa3e292595976c6d0077fec9c43b0af6a13c165abbd754fa7fcbe7.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(t)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be $f(t),$ where $u$ denotes the unit step function?" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321063 1072 #uuid "488bdada-725c-59e2-b765-d0800d43ce1e" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321063 1074 "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nu(t)={\\begin{aligned}1,\\,t\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\,t<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=u(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/38/385fe662259d5c933171b68f6b4b7227051921c3327da8f1ca10ff3bd6c5da3f.png)\n\nNotice that to transform the function $y=u(t)$ to the given function $y=f(t),$ we perform the following transformations:\n\n- First, we reflect $y=u(t)$ in the $y$-axis, which gives $y=u(-t).$\n\n- Next, we shift by one unit to the left, giving $y=u(-(t+1))=u(-t-1).$\n\n- Finally, we scale by a scale factor of $4$ parallel to the $y$-axis, giving $y=4u(-t-1).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(t)=4u(-t-1).$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321064 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320644 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321064 1069 "q-35292" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321064 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321064 1071 "Determine the dimension of\n$\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}.$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321064 1072 #uuid "8505e165-2993-5e50-9039-0c1c0e794caf" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321064 1074 "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -7 \\\\ -5 & 2 & -9 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 22 & -44 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{3}{22}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 22 & -44 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, the dimension of the span is $2.$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321065 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320597 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321065 1069 "q-352924" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321065 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321065 1071 "Compute the arc length of $y=\\frac{{e}^{4x}+{e}^{-4x}}{8}$ for $x\\in [0,\\frac{1}{2}].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321065 1072 #uuid "9fe919cc-2020-562f-9dce-f16756b25954" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321065 1074 "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{{e}^{4x}+{e}^{-4x}}{8},$ $a=0,$ and $b=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{e}^{4x}-{e}^{-4x}}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{{e}^{4x}-{e}^{-4x}}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{e}^{8x}-2+{e}^{-8x}}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{e}^{8x}+2+{e}^{-8x}}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{({e}^{4x}+{e}^{-4x})^{2}}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{4x}+{e}^{-4x}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\frac{{e}^{4x}+{e}^{-4x}}{2}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{{e}^{4x}+{e}^{-4x}}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{e}^{4x}-{e}^{-4x}}{8})|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{e}^{4(1/2)}-{e}^{-4(1/2)}}{8}]-[\\frac{{e}^{4(0)}-{e}^{-4(0)}}{8}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}({e}^{2}-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{4}-1}{8{e}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321066 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320332 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321066 1069 "q-352925" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321066 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321066 1071 "Compute the arc length of $y=\\frac{{e}^{3x}+{e}^{-3x}}{6}$ for $x\\in [0,1].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321066 1072 #uuid "13f6ace1-1999-5d34-ac80-deb1a0dcd5d5" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321066 1074 "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{{e}^{3x}+{e}^{-3x}}{6},$ $a=0,$ and $b=1.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{e}^{3x}-{e}^{-3x}}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{{e}^{3x}-{e}^{-3x}}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{e}^{6x}-2+{e}^{-6x}}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{e}^{6x}+2+{e}^{-6x}}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{({e}^{3x}+{e}^{-3x})^{2}}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3x}+{e}^{-3x}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\frac{{e}^{3x}+{e}^{-3x}}{2}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{e}^{3x}+{e}^{-3x}}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{e}^{3x}-{e}^{-3x}}{6})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{e}^{3(1)}-{e}^{-3(1)}}{6}]-[\\frac{{e}^{3(0)}-{e}^{-3(0)}}{6}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}({e}^{3}-\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{6}-1}{6{e}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321067 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320422 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321067 1069 "q-35297" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321067 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321067 1071 "Determine the dimension of\n$\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]}.$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321067 1072 #uuid "2472ea9f-8e8e-5b80-8c7c-945a5a43c650" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321067 1074 "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}5 & -10 \\\\ 4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{4}{5}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 6 \\\\ 3 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{3}{5}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, the dimension of the span is $2.$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321068 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320602 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321068 1069 "q-35298" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321068 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321068 1071 "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]})=$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321068 1072 #uuid "2304859f-a364-5093-b7c5-56d02eda6877" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321068 1074 "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 6 & -10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $1$ pivot column (or alternatively, it has exactly $1$ non-zero row). \n\nTherefore, \n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]})=1.$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321069 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320553 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321069 1069 "q-35299" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321069 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321069 1071 "Determine the dimension of\n$\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}.$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321069 1072 #uuid "9e1095e2-b747-574e-a018-9f92abb5fb55" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321069 1074 "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & -\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{3}{10}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $3$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $3$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, the dimension of the span is $3.$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321070 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320333 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321070 1069 "q-353019" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321070 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321070 1071 "Consider the arc of the curve $y=1+{x}^{2}$ that lies between the lines $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Calculate the surface area $S$ generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321070 1072 #uuid "a56a5b68-c2a3-5c78-b4ca-8aa4e273c927" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321070 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1+{x}^{2})=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}=4{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThen, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}x\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u=1+4{x}^{2}.$ Then, $8x\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.$\n\nWe also need to compute new limits:\n\n| **$x$** | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}x\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{8}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{u}^{1/2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}{[\\frac{2{u}^{3/2}}{3}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}[(2)^{3/2}-(1)^{3/2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}(2\\sqrt{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}(2\\sqrt{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321071 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320660 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321071 1069 "q-35302" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321071 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321071 1071 "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]})=$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321071 1072 #uuid "2a41f8fd-4b7f-554c-b544-a6304735d193" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321071 1074 "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{5}{2} & 5 & -\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, \n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]})=2.$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321072 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320436 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321072 1069 "q-35305" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321072 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321072 1071 "Let $A$ be a $3\\times 5$ matrix. If $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$, find $\\text{dim(Null}(A)).$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321072 1072 #uuid "5fad5527-7b65-5529-b82c-0602148a162d" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321072 1074 "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=5,$ and since $B$ is formed by $3$ vectors, $\\text{rank}(A)=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 3+\\text{nullity}(A) & =5 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =5-3 \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321073 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320293 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321073 1069 "q-353136" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321073 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321073 1071 "![](images/f7/f7eebd794b43da16658750db0df3a4d4c4b894ddb80f84795ea483aa4d579f56.png)\n\nIf the graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above, then\n\n${\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321073 1072 #uuid "61749f10-120d-515e-846d-ecff78e32dbd" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321073 1074 "We divide the interval of integration into $3$ regions: the arc of the semi-circle for $x\\in [-4,0],$ the line segment for $x\\in [0,4),$ and the line segment for $x\\in (4,6].$ Using the additive property of integrals, we write\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,0].$ The corresponding region is a lower semi-circle of radius $2$, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi (2)^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{1}=-2\\pi .\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,4).$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (4,6].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4+2)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{1}+{A}_{2}+{A}_{3} \\\\  & =-2\\pi +8+6 \\\\  & =14-2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321074 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320544 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321074 1069 "q-353146" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321074 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321074 1071 "Find the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of the geometric series \n\n$$\n1.5+1.2+0.96+0.768+⋯.\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n${S}_{\\infty }=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321074 1072 #uuid "4eb9b2fe-1314-5f61-80a1-c99a90d06bf9" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321074 1074 "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFor the given geometric series, the first term is ${a}_{1}=1.5$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{1.2}{1.5}=0.8.\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{1.5}{1-0.8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1.5}{0.2} \\\\  & =7.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321075 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320640 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321075 1069 "q-353155" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321075 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321075 1071 "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{4}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{4}x)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=3x$ for $x\\in [-4,4).$ The Fourier series of the $8$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of ${b}_{5}.$\n\n${b}_{5}=$ {{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321075 1072 #uuid "4bdcfdd0-602f-5c57-a72a-05077e84573d" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321075 1074 "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the sine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=4$ and $f(x)=3x$ for $x\\in (-4,4).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{b}_{5}=\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}3x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=3x\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=3 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=-\\frac{4}{5\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{5} & =\\frac{1}{4}(uv\\,{|}_{-4}^{4}-{\\int }_{-4}^{4}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(-\\frac{4}{5\\pi }{[3x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}x)]}_{-4}^{4}+\\frac{12}{5\\pi }{\\int }_{-4}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\pi }(-{[3x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}x)]}_{-4}^{4}+3{\\int }_{-4}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\pi }(-((3(4))\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi )-(3(-4))\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-5\\pi ))+\\frac{12}{5\\pi }{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}x)]}_{-4}^{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\pi }(24+\\frac{12}{5\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-5\\pi ))) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\pi }\\cdot 24 \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{5\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321076 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320309 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321076 1069 "q-353156" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321076 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321076 1071 "Find the radius of convergence $R$ of the following series:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{n}{x}^{n+1}}{(n+4)^{2}}=\\frac{x}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}+\\frac{{3}^{2}{x}^{3}}{{6}^{2}}+\\frac{{3}^{3}{x}^{4}}{{7}^{2}}+⋯\n$$\n\n$R=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321076 1072 #uuid "899d8678-7a47-590b-bb00-d50d6baef1cd" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321076 1074 "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{3}^{n}{x}^{n+1}}{(n+4)^{2}}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{3}^{n+1}{x}^{n+2}}{(n+5)^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{(n+4)^{2}}{{3}^{n}{x}^{n+1}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{3x(n+4)^{2}}{(n+5)^{2}}| \\\\  & =3|x|\\cdot {lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(n+4)^{2}}{(n+5)^{2}} \\\\  & =3|x|\\cdot {lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{(1+\\frac{4}{n})}^{2}}{{(1+\\frac{5}{n})}^{2}} \\\\  & =3|x|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3|x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied if \n\n$$\n3|x|<1\\,⇒\\,|x|<\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\frac{1}{3}.$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321077 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320181 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321077 1069 "q-353158" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321077 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321077 1071 "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}x)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=5x$ for $x\\in [-2,2).$ The Fourier series of the $4$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of ${b}_{3}.$\n\n${b}_{3}=$ {{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321077 1072 #uuid "f0c22213-3f1c-5033-a8a8-dae972b44b5d" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321077 1074 "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the sine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=2$ and $f(x)=5x$ for $x\\in (-2,2).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{b}_{3}=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}(5x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=5x\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=5 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=-\\frac{2}{3\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(uv\\,{|}_{-2}^{2}-{\\int }_{-2}^{2}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{2}{3\\pi }{[5x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x)]}_{-2}^{2}+\\frac{10}{3\\pi }{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }(-{[5x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x)]}_{-2}^{2}+5{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }(-(5(2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi )-5(-2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3\\pi ))+\\frac{10}{3\\pi }{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x)]}_{-2}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }(20+\\frac{10}{3\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-3\\pi ))) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }\\cdot 20 \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{3\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321078 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320456 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321078 1069 "q-35316" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321078 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321078 1071 "Consider the matrices given below. Find $\\,\\text{dim(Null}(A))+\\text{dim(Null}(B))+\\text{dim(Null}(C)).$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321078 1072 #uuid "da3efbc4-2fa4-54a8-9aa9-e1c2c723490a" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321078 1074 "The dimension of the null space of a matrix is the number of non-pivot columns in the matrix. \n\nAlso, all of the given matrices are in row echelon form.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- The matrix $A$ has $3$ pivot columns and no non-pivot column. So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(A))=0.\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ has $1$ pivot column and $1$ non-pivot column (namely, the $2$nd column). So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(B))=1.\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ has $3$ pivot columns and $1$ non-pivot column (namely, the $4$th column). So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(C))=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(A))+\\text{dim(Null}(B))+\\text{dim(Null}(C))=0+1+1=2.\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321079 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320705 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321079 1069 "q-353168" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321079 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321079 1071 "Find the radius of convergence $R$ of the following series:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}(3x)^{n}}{(n+2)(n+4)}=\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 4}-\\frac{3x}{3\\cdot 5}+\\frac{{3}^{2}{x}^{2}}{4\\cdot 6}-\\frac{{3}^{3}{x}^{3}}{5\\cdot 7}+\\frac{{3}^{4}{x}^{4}}{6\\cdot 8}+⋯\\,.\n$$\n\n$R=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321079 1072 #uuid "b3b3de96-fde7-5b69-8a66-4f83bfa93c6f" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321079 1074 "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}(3x)^{n}}{(n+2)(n+4)}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(3x)^{n+1}}{(n+3)(n+5)}\\cdot \\frac{(n+2)(n+4)}{(-1)^{n}(3x)^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{-3x\\,(n+2)(n+4)}{(n+3)(n+5)}| \\\\  & =3|x|\\cdot {lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(n+2)(n+4)}{(n+3)(n+5)} \\\\  & =3|x|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3|x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied if \n\n$$\n3|x|<1\\,⇒\\,|x|<\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\frac{1}{3}.$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321080 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320512 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321080 1069 "q-35317" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321080 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321080 1071 "If $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,$ then $\\text{nullity}(A)=$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321080 1072 #uuid "5c9fb6cf-01de-5080-82f7-0128ba4fbceb" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321080 1074 "Recall that by definition, $\\text{nullity}(A)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(A)).$\n\nTo find $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(A))$, we reduce $A$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 9 & 28\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-7){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 23 & 56\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix has $3$ pivot columns and $1$ non-pivot columns. \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(A))=1.\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321081 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320214 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321081 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320454 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321081 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320469 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321081 1069 "q-353173" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321081 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321081 1071 "Find the interval of convergence for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(2x)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}}=\\frac{2x}{{5}^{3/2}}-\\frac{(2x)^{2}}{{6}^{3/2}}+\\frac{(2x)^{3}}{{7}^{3/2}}-\\frac{(2x)^{4}}{{8}^{3/2}}+⋯\\,.\n$$\n\nThe interval of convergence is\n{{field-1}}\n$\\le x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}." 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321081 1072 #uuid "fb70248c-d191-522a-acca-d1658cbddb31" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321081 1074 "To apply the ratio test we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(2x)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n+2}(2x)^{n+1}}{(n+5)^{3/2}}\\cdot \\frac{(n+4)^{3/2}}{(-1)^{n+1}(2x)^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{-2x(n+4)^{3/2}}{(n+5)^{3/2}}| \\\\  & =2|x|\\cdot {lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{n+4}{n+5})}^{3/2} \\\\  & =2|x|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2|x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for $2|x|<1,$ or equivalently $|x|<\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, the radius of convergence is $\\frac{1}{2},$ and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1.$ So, when $|x|=\\frac{1}{2},$ we do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(2x)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}\\cdot (1)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{5}^{3/2}}-\\frac{1}{{6}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{7}^{3/2}}-\\frac{1}{{8}^{3/2}}+⋯\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the alternating series test.  So the endpoint $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ is included in our interval.\n\n- When $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(2x)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(-1)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{2n+1}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-(\\frac{1}{{5}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{6}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{7}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{8}^{3/2}}+⋯)\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe notice that each term of this series ${a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{(n+4)^{3/2}}$ is smaller than ${b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{{n}^{3/2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(n+4)^{3/2}}\\le \\frac{1}{{n}^{3/2}}\n$$\n\nSince ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{3/2}}$ is convergent, the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(n+4)^{3/2}}$ is convergent by the comparison test. So the endpoint $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is included in our interval.\n\nTherefore, the interval of convergence is $[-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}],$ or equivalently, $-\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321082 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320389 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321082 1069 "q-35318" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321082 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321082 1071 "If $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 9 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 6 & -3 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,$ then $\\text{nullity}(A)=$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321082 1072 #uuid "687cbaa7-684a-507a-80f4-6f515d95364b" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321082 1074 "Recall that by definition, $\\text{nullity}(A)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(A)).$\n\nTo find $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(A))$, we reduce $A$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 9 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 6 & -3 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 9 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 13 \\\\ 6 & -3 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 9 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 13 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix has $3$ pivot columns and no non-pivot columns. \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(A))=0.\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321083 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320598 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321083 1069 "q-353180" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321083 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321083 1071 "The amount of a rare plant species $A(t)$ after $t$ years can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=0.072A.\n$$\n\nIf there are currently $320$ plants, calculate the amount after $9$ years. Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\nThe amount of the plant species after $9$ years is approximately \n{{field-1}}\nplants." 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321083 1072 #uuid "bc7e694f-b4b0-55d0-bb20-74aed5966295" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321083 1074 "We model the amount using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=0.072A.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nA(t)={A}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.072t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${A}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that there are currently $320$ plants, so we have ${A}_{0}=320.$\n\nTherefore, the amount $A$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nA(t)=320\\,{e}^{0.072t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the amount when $t=9,$ we substitute $t=9$ into the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(9) & =320\\,{e}^{0.072\\cdot 9} \\\\  & =320\\,{e}^{0.648} \\\\  & ≈611.53\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding our answer to the nearest integer, we conclude that the amount of the plant species after $9$ years is approximately $612$ plants." 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321084 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320381 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321084 1069 "q-353183" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321084 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321084 1071 "Compute the arc length of $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)$ for $x\\in [1,2].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321084 1072 #uuid "33ee3983-1139-50d7-b7d0-6d2d63a4db56" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321084 1074 "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x),$ $a=1,$ and $b=2.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{1}{x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{4}+8{x}^{2}+16}{16{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{({x}^{2}+4)^{2}}{16{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{4x}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{x}{4}+\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{2}^{2}}{8}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2]-[\\frac{{1}^{2}}{8}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡1] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321085 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320647 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321085 1069 "q-353186" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321085 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321085 1071 "The number of cells in a petri dish $C(t)$ after $t$ hours can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=C.\n$$\n\nIf there are currently $650$ cells in the petri dish, calculate the number of cells after $2$ hours. Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\nThe number of cells after $2$ hours is approximately \n{{field-1}}\ncells." 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321085 1072 #uuid "dd183ed4-aa74-5e82-bf56-bd34f83720dc" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321085 1074 "We model the number of cells using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=C.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nC(t)={C}_{0}\\,{e}^{t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that there are currently $650$ cells in the petri dish, so we have ${C}_{0}=650.$\n\nTherefore, the number of cells $C$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nC(t)=650\\,{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the number of cells when $t=2,$ we substitute $t=2$ into the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(2) & =650\\,{e}^{2} \\\\  & ≈4802.89\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding our answer to the nearest integer, we conclude that the number of cells after $2$ hours is approximately $4803$ cells." 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321086 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320378 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321086 1069 "q-353189" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321086 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321086 1071 "The number of insects in a controlled habitat $H(t)$ after $t$ months can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=2H.\n$$\n\nIf there are currently $120$ insects in the habitat, calculate the number of insects after $2$ months. Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\nThe number of insects after $2$ months is approximately \n{{field-1}}\ninsects." 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321086 1072 #uuid "8f062774-f5b6-5715-9510-abfa22379f04" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321086 1074 "We model the number of insects using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=2H.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nH(t)={H}_{0}\\,{e}^{2t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${H}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that there are currently $120$ insects in the habitat, so we have ${H}_{0}=120.$\n\nTherefore, the number of insects $H$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nH(t)=120\\,{e}^{2t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the number of insects when $t=2,$ we substitute $t=2$ into the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}H(2) & =120\\,{e}^{2\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =120\\,{e}^{4} \\\\  & ≈6551.78\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding our answer to the nearest integer, we conclude that the number of insects after $2$ months is approximately $6552$ insects." 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321087 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320530 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321087 1069 "q-353191" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321087 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321087 1071 "Compute the arc length of $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)$ for $x\\in [1,3].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321087 1072 #uuid "69eeffe4-ca91-54ed-830e-b460d5fd1e48" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321087 1074 "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x),$ $a=1,$ and $b=3.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{1}{x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{4}+8{x}^{2}+16}{16{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{({x}^{2}+4)^{2}}{16{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{4x}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}(\\frac{x}{4}+\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{3}^{2}}{8}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3]-[\\frac{{1}^{2}}{8}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡1] \\\\  & =1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321088 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320385 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321088 1069 "q-353197" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321088 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321088 1071 "The population of a bacterial culture $P(t)$ after $t$ hours can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=-P.\n$$\n\nIf there are currently $30$ bacteria in the culture, calculate the population after $3$ hours. Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\nThe population after $3$ hours is approximately \n{{field-1}}\nbacteria." 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321088 1072 #uuid "2bd3368e-0e3f-5373-9613-b9232c8c8ce1" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321088 1074 "We model the population using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=-P.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)={P}_{0}\\,{e}^{-t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that there are currently $30$ bacteria in the culture, so we have ${P}_{0}=30.$\n\nTherefore, the population $P$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nP(t)=30\\,{e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the population when $t=3,$ we substitute $t=3$ into the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3) & =30\\,{e}^{-1\\cdot 3} \\\\  & =30\\,{e}^{-3} \\\\  & ≈1.49\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding our answer to the nearest integer, we conclude that the population after $3$ hours is approximately $1$ bacterium." 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321089 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320451 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321089 1069 "q-353210" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321089 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321089 1071 "The number of bacteria $N=N(t)$ in a laboratory culture $t$ hours after observation can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=kN\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is the growth rate. If there were $500$ bacteria initially, and $N(3)=900$, calculate the value of the growth rate $k$ to the nearest percent.\n\n$k≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$%$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321089 1072 #uuid "5ffc0670-7032-5618-9938-5d5033bcb5b3" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321089 1074 "We model the number of bacteria using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=kN.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nN(t)={N}_{0}\\,{e}^{kt},\n$$\n\nwhere ${N}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that there were $500$ bacteria initially, so we have ${N}_{0}=500.$\n\nTherefore, the number of bacteria $N$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nN(t)=500\\,{e}^{kt}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $k,$ we substitute $N(3)=900$ into the equation above and solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(3) & =500\\,{e}^{k\\cdot 3} \\\\ 900 & =500\\,{e}^{3k} \\\\ {e}^{3k} & =\\frac{900}{500} \\\\ {e}^{3k} & =\\frac{9}{5} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{3k}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{5}) \\\\ 3k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{5}) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{5}) \\\\ k & ≈0.1959\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k≈20%,$ rounded to the nearest percent." 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321090 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320200 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321090 1069 "q-353211" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321090 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321090 1071 "The number of subscribers $S=S(t)$ to a website $t$ months after a marketing campaign can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=kS\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is the growth rate. If there were $12\\,000$ subscribers initially, and $S(2)=15\\,000$, calculate the value of the growth rate $k$ to the nearest percent.\n\n$k≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$%$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321090 1072 #uuid "3f112590-ba59-50ed-81c5-89311d726307" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321090 1074 "We model the number of subscribers using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=kS.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nS(t)={S}_{0}\\,{e}^{kt},\n$$\n\nwhere ${S}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that there were $12\\,000$ subscribers initially, so we have ${S}_{0}=12\\,000.$\n\nTherefore, the number of subscribers $S$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nS(t)=12\\,000\\,{e}^{kt}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $k,$ we substitute $S(2)=15\\,000$ into the equation above and solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(2) & =12\\,000\\,{e}^{k\\cdot 2} \\\\ 15\\,000 & =12\\,000\\,{e}^{2k} \\\\ {e}^{2k} & =\\frac{15\\,000}{12\\,000} \\\\ {e}^{2k} & =\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2k}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\ 2k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\ k & ≈0.111\\,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k≈11%,$ rounded to the nearest percent." 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321091 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320659 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321091 1069 "q-353217" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321091 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321091 1071 "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent line to the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )$ at $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{8}$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ), \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$m=$ \n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321091 1072 #uuid "5a9a6fe7-11ca-57f2-9394-dc75bd8e2e74" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321091 1074 "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nWe have,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the slope $m$ of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =3\\pi /8} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{8})}{{x}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321092 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320534 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321092 1069 "q-353218" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321092 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321092 1071 "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent line to the polar curve $r=1+\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\theta )$ at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+{sec}^{2}⁡(\\theta ), \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$m=$ \n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321092 1072 #uuid "286990a3-3f35-503f-8eaf-0cc7f8896a43" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321092 1074 "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nWe have,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})+{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+{2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+4 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the slope $m$ of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /3} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})}{{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{9}{2})}{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =-3\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321093 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320252 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321093 1069 "q-353226" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321093 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321093 1071 "![](images/06/064e75694d82bed2177df55e2e66c3343a2c0396cd54f5d4304f952a01abb219.png)\n\nFor the polar curve $r=\\theta $ (shown above) evaluate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$   at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n${\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /2}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321093 1072 #uuid "ffacd058-e0e5-543f-8b9e-1afffd584455" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321093 1074 "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the rule for differentiating parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the numerator and denominator at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =1+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =0-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321094 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320625 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321094 1069 "q-353227" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321094 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321094 1071 "For the polar curve $r=13,$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ as a function of $\\theta .$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321094 1072 #uuid "01fd9975-36b2-5473-97e0-a0dc84b811f7" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321094 1074 "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=13,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =13\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=13\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =-13\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=13\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{13\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{-13\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321095 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320511 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321095 1069 "q-353229" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321095 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321095 1071 "For the polar curve $r=22,$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ as a function of $\\theta .$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321095 1072 #uuid "e83e8aed-b191-51dc-a17a-33ba5de6ba85" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321095 1074 "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=22,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =22\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=22\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =-22\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=22\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{22\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{-22\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321096 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320453 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321096 1069 "q-35323" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321096 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321096 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=4x+3b$, where $b$ is a constant." 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321096 1072 #uuid "fc796b8b-9fd5-5962-9783-c6ca55e2aaea" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321096 1074 "To find the inverse of a function, we follow these steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Replace $f(x)$ with $y$:\n\n$$\ny=4x+3b\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Swap $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\nx=4y+3b\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4y+3b \\\\ x-3b & =4y \\\\ \\frac{x-3b}{4} & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Replace $y$ with ${f}^{-1}(x)$:\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{x-3b}{4}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321097 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320648 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321097 1069 "q-353232" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321097 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321097 1071 "![](images/c1/c1fe71fe51ae93fc2e78b52b1d3f1c270ab05d10c317345bf1440b13c570483f.png)\n\nFor the polar curve $r=30$ (shown above), evaluate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,{|}_{\\theta \\,=\\,3\\pi /4}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321097 1072 #uuid "cb4d30fd-268a-5c12-85e7-7c896536b59c" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321097 1074 "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=30,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =30\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=30\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =-30\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=30\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{30\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{(-30\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,{|}_{\\theta \\,=\\,3\\pi /4} & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321098 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320169 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321098 1069 "q-353244" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321098 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321098 1071 "![](images/89/89ca909a3e3628e83e6aa4b2a20049ca33bd0436e130017caa9266fbea259022.png)\n\nFor the polar curve $r=\\frac{\\theta }{2\\pi },$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ as a function of $\\theta .$\n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ |" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321098 1072 #uuid "bf25c5ed-77c5-5921-8e90-bb9948e9a149" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321098 1074 "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=\\frac{\\theta }{2\\pi },$ we get\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\theta }{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{\\theta }{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ).$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\frac{\\theta }{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }{(\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[(\\theta )^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{′}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\frac{\\theta }{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }{(\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[(\\theta )^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{′}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]}{\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321099 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320299 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321099 1069 "q-353245" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321099 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321099 1071 "![](images/7b/7b11eccd4d3e5e1e90565219ce9b5098cd5aaeafe67c64d5e148245e17a6d355.png)\n\nFor the polar curve $r=\\frac{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{3\\pi },$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ as a function of $\\theta .$\n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ |" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321099 1072 #uuid "910d46f2-1fea-5ef6-b401-5fcd3e6242d2" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321099 1074 "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=\\frac{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{3\\pi },$ we get\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{3\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{3\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ).$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\frac{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{3\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }{((2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }[(2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +(2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{′}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +(2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }[{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -(2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }[{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\frac{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{3\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }{((2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }[(2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{′}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }[2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }[2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{3\\pi }[2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )]}{\\frac{1}{3\\pi }[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321100 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320706 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321100 1069 "q-353249" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321100 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321100 1071 "For the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ as a function of $\\theta .$\n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ |" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321100 1072 #uuid "2c4f7762-591a-5efe-8fa5-462fda201bae" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321100 1074 "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,$ we get\n\n$$\nx=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ):$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={((\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)}^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={((\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)}^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321101 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320199 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321101 1069 "q-353253" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321101 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321101 1071 "The growth rate of the number of residents using a new fitness app, $P(t),$ in a small town is jointly proportional to the number of residents using the app at time $t,$ measured in months, and the difference between the town’s maximum potential users and the number of residents currently using the app.\n\nGiven that the constant of proportionality is $0.005$ and the maximum potential number of users is $800,$ find an expression for the number of residents using the app after $t$ months, given that there were $100$ users initially.\n\n| $P(t)\\,=\\,\\,$ | $800$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $1\\,+\\,$ {{field-1}} |  |" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321101 1072 #uuid "9ace8231-6062-5814-8e5b-1982354dbc72" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321101 1074 "Since the number of residents using the app grows at a rate that is *jointly* proportional to the number of residents using the app at time $t$ and the difference between the maximum potential number of users and the number currently using the app, the number of residents using the app follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.005,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=800.$ Therefore, the growth is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.005\\,P(800-P).\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{800}{1+C{e}^{-(800\\cdot 0.005)t}}=\\frac{800}{1+C{e}^{-4t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=100$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{800}{1+C{e}^{-4\\cdot 0}} & =100 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{800}{100} \\\\ C & =8-1 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{800}{1+7{e}^{-4t}}.\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321102 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320357 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321102 1069 "q-353254" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321102 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321102 1071 "The growth rate of the number of students enrolled in an after-school program, $P(t),$ is jointly proportional to the number of students enrolled at time $t,$ measured in weeks, and the difference between the program’s maximum enrollment and the number of students enrolled.\n\nGiven that the constant of proportionality is $0.004$ and the maximum enrollment is $500,$ find an expression for the number of students enrolled after $t$ weeks, given that there were $50$ students enrolled initially.\n\n| $P(t)\\,=\\,\\,$ | $500$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $1\\,+\\,$ {{field-1}} |  |" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321102 1072 #uuid "692933aa-c26c-54b1-9255-1848554ec812" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321102 1074 "Since the number of students enrolled grows at a rate that is *jointly* proportional to the number of students enrolled at time $t$ and the difference between the maximum enrollment and the number of students enrolled, the number of students enrolled follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.004,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=500.$ Therefore, the growth is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.004\\,P(500-P).\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{500}{1+C{e}^{-(500\\cdot 0.004)t}}=\\frac{500}{1+C{e}^{-2t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=50$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{500}{1+C{e}^{-2\\cdot 0}} & =50 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{500}{50} \\\\ C & =10-1 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{500}{1+9{e}^{-2t}}.\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321103 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320676 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321103 1069 "q-353256" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321103 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321103 1071 "The growth rate of the number of trees in a reforestation area, $P(t),$ is jointly proportional to the number of trees at time $t,$ measured in years, and the difference between the area’s carrying capacity and the number of trees.\n\nGiven that the constant of proportionality is $\\frac{1}{3000}$ and the carrying capacity is $1500,$ find an expression for the number of trees after $t$ years, given that there were $300$ trees in the area initially.\n\n| $P(t)\\,=\\,\\,$ | $1500$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $1\\,+\\,$ {{field-1}} |  |" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321103 1072 #uuid "4a457f5a-fd05-5ec9-b464-87d60b0040e3" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321103 1074 "Since the number of trees grows at a rate that is *jointly* proportional to the number of trees at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the number of trees, the number of trees follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=\\frac{1}{3000},$ and the carrying capacity is $a=1500.$ Therefore, the growth is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=\\frac{1}{3000}\\,P(1500-P).\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{1500}{1+C{e}^{-(1500\\cdot \\frac{1}{3000})t}}=\\frac{1500}{1+C{e}^{-t/2}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=300$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1500}{1+C{e}^{-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0}} & =300 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{1500}{300} \\\\ C & =5-1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{1500}{1+4{e}^{-t/2}}.\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321104 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320352 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321104 1069 "q-353260" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321104 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321104 1071 "The population $P(t)$ of insects in a greenhouse follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.6P-0.002{P}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, measured in days. If there were $40$ insects in the greenhouse initially, how many insects were in the greenhouse after $3$ days? Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\nAfter $3$ days, approximately\n{{field-1}} insects were in the greenhouse." 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321104 1072 #uuid "f5ba51a1-46ff-5704-9961-5ad994533c3c" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321104 1074 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}},\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe start by factoring the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{dt} & =0.6P-0.002{P}^{2} \\\\  & =0.002P(300-P).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the constant of proportionality is $k=0.002,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=300.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{300}{1+C{e}^{-(300\\cdot 0.002)t}}=\\frac{300}{1+C{e}^{-0.6t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=40$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{300}{1+C{e}^{-0.6(0)}} & =40 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{300}{40} \\\\ C & =7.5-1 \\\\  & =6.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{300}{1+6.5{e}^{-0.6t}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $t=3$ into the expression for $P(t),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3) & =\\frac{300}{1+6.5{e}^{-0.6(3)}} \\\\  & ≈145.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $3$ days, approximately $145$ insects were in the greenhouse." 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321105 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320317 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321105 1069 "q-353261" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321105 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321105 1071 "The population $P(t)$ of customers signed up for a grocery store’s rewards program follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.35P-0.0007{P}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, measured in months. If there were $120$ customers signed up initially, how many customers were signed up after $7$ months? Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\nAfter $7$ months, approximately\n{{field-1}} customers were signed up." 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321105 1072 #uuid "f4a1bc9c-ccc0-5ad5-afe3-c9ce6d877291" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321105 1074 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}},\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe start by factoring the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{dt} & =0.35P-0.0007{P}^{2} \\\\  & =0.0007P(500-P).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the constant of proportionality is $k=0.0007,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=500.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{500}{1+C{e}^{-(500\\cdot 0.0007)t}}=\\frac{500}{1+C{e}^{-0.35t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=120$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{500}{1+C{e}^{-0.35(0)}} & =120 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{500}{120} \\\\ C & =\\frac{25}{6}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{500}{1+\\frac{19}{6}{e}^{-0.35t}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $t=7$ into the expression for $P(t),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(7) & =\\frac{500}{1+\\frac{19}{6}{e}^{-0.35(7)}} \\\\  & ≈393.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $7$ months, approximately $393$ customers were signed up." 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321106 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320622 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321106 1069 "q-353263" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321106 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321106 1071 "The population $P(t)$ of customers using a new ride-share service in a city follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.18P-0.000\\,12{P}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, measured in months. If there were $900$ customers using the service initially, how many customers were using the service after $8$ months? Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\nAfter $8$ months, approximately\n{{field-1}} customers were using the service." 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321106 1072 #uuid "1ecc4007-5828-54a3-bc49-c69c66b432ce" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321106 1074 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}},\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe start by factoring the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{dt} & =0.18P-0.000\\,12{P}^{2} \\\\  & =0.000\\,12P(1500-P).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the constant of proportionality is $k=0.00012,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=1500.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{1500}{1+C{e}^{-(1500\\cdot 0.000\\,12)t}}=\\frac{1500}{1+C{e}^{-0.18t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=900$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1500}{1+C{e}^{-0.18(0)}} & =900 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{1500}{900} \\\\ C & =\\frac{5}{3}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{1500}{1+\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{-0.18t}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $t=8$ into the expression for $P(t),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(8) & =\\frac{1500}{1+\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{-0.18(8)}} \\\\  & ≈1295.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $8$ months, approximately $1295$ customers were using the service." 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321107 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320243 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321107 1069 "q-353287" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321107 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321107 1071 "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{y}},\\,y(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nUse the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ at $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ to approximate $y(\\frac{3}{4}).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$y(\\frac{3}{4})≈$  \n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321107 1072 #uuid "dc6357fd-9ea7-5406-b8cc-4035a2ad90d5" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321107 1074 "The tangent line approximation to $y(x)$ at the point $a=\\frac{1}{2}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(a)+{y}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =y(\\frac{1}{2})+{y}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2})(x-\\frac{1}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the initial condition, we know that $y(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{4}.$ We also know that ${y}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{y}},$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2}) & =-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $y(\\frac{1}{2})$ and ${y}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2})$ into our expression for $L(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(\\frac{1}{2})+{y}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2})(x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+(-1)(x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate $y(\\frac{3}{4})$ using $L(\\frac{3}{4}).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{3}{4}) & ≈L(\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321108 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320375 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321108 1069 "q-353288" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321108 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321108 1071 "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{y},\\,y(1)=2.\n$$\n\nUse the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ at $x=1$ to approximate $y(\\frac{3}{2}).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$y(\\frac{3}{2})≈$  \n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321108 1072 #uuid "e74cc3fc-655c-51c1-a449-0108494451f0" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321108 1074 "The tangent line approximation to $y(x)$ at the point $a=1$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(a)+{y}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =y(1)+{y}^{′}(1)(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the initial condition, we know that $y(1)=2.$ We also know that ${y}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{1}{y},$ so\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(1)=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $y(1)$ and ${y}^{′}(1)$ into our expression for $L(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(1)+{y}^{′}(1)(x-1) \\\\  & =2+(-\\frac{1}{2})(x-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate $y(\\frac{3}{2})$ using $L(\\frac{3}{2}).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{3}{2}) & ≈L(\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{4}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321109 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320188 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321109 1069 "q-353294" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321109 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321109 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x+2y,\\,y(1)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method with step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{4}.$ Fill in the empty box in the column for $Δy$ in the table below using this method. Do not round the answer.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321109 1072 #uuid "a99fa9c0-7aae-5fb7-bab4-e957eccf74ef" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321109 1074 "First, because the initial condition is $y(1)=1,$ we fill $y=1$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+2y \\\\  & =1+2(1) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $$ |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =3(\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ |" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321110 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320699 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321110 1069 "q-353296" 13194139533420 17592186045585 tru