|\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x(2y-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot 1-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{5}{4}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1)≈\\frac{5}{4}.$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321116 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320180 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321116 1069 "q-353340" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321116 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321116 1071 "![](images/e0/e0b1dc90950ba127e90fce400bc7ed1b6e8dd8e710ff880ab5949521b02a258a.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve (shown above)\n\n$$\nx=4{e}^{-t},\\,y={t}^{2}+6t,\\,0\\le t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\nfind the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx}{|}_{t\\,=\\,1}=$  \n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [