egration are from ${y}_{1}=0$ to ${y}_{2}=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{4}{3}{y}^{3/2})=2\\sqrt{y}\\,⇒\\,{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}=4y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{1+4y}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the substitution \n\n$$\nu=1+4y\\,⇒\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}y,\\,y=\\frac{u-1}{4},\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{1+4y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{9}\\frac{u-1}{4}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}{\\int }_{1}^{9}(u-1)\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}{\\int }_{1}^{9}({u}^{3/2}-{u}^{1/2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}(\\frac{2}{5}{u}^{5/2}-\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2})\\,{|}_{1}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}((\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {9}^{5/2}-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {9}^{3/2})-(\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {1}^{5/2}-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {1}^{3/2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}((\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot 243-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 27)-(\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{2}{3})) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}(\\frac{396}{5}+\\frac{4}{15}) \\\\  & =\\frac{149\\pi }{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321129 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320271 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321129 1069 "q-353412" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321129 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321129 1071 "For the polar curve $r={e}^{\\theta },$ evaluate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at $\\theta =0.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,{|}_{\\theta \\,=\\,0}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321129 1072 #uuid "91159a14-bb15-575a-a44c-36c600c2badd" 13194139533420 17592186045585 t