 [17592186321137 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321137 1071 "A bookstore sells $27$ copies of a particular novel every day. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the number of copies $N(t)$ remaining in stock? Assume that the time $t$ is measured in days.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=$  \n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321137 1072 #uuid "5afeefec-1d93-584b-b1a4-e7b70d6e5276" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321137 1074 "If the number of copies remaining in stock is $N,$ then the rate of change of the number of copies is $\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t},$ where $t$ is the time in days.\n\nSince $27$ copies are sold each day, the rate of change of the number of copies is given by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=-27.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is negative because the number of copies remaining is *decreasing*." 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321138 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320714 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321138 1069 "q-353464" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321138 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321138 1071 "Iodine-131 has a half-life of $8.02$ days. A sample of iodine-131 has an initial mass of $7.2\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ Rounded to **one decimal place**, what is the mass of the sample after $12$ days?\n\nThe mass of the sample after $12$ days is approximately\n{{field-1}}\n$\\mu \\text{g}.$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321138 1072 #uuid "30e29f26-7f67-5a4d-9775-f9729308f554" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321138 1074 "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=8.02$ days, we have\n\n$$\n8.02=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{8.02}.\n$$\n\nAlso, since ${M}_{0}=7.2\\,\\mu \\text{g},$ we have the particular solution\n\n$$\nM(t)=7.2{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/8.02}.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nM(12)=7.2{e}^{-12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/8.02}≈2.6,\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the mass of the sample remaining after $12$ days is approximately $2.6\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321139 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320240 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321139 1069 "q-353477" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321139 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321139 1071 "The number $y(t)$ of people who have participated in a viral trend on social media follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=1250y-5{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=40,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in hours, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible number of participants?\n\nThe maximum possible number of participants is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321139 1072 #uuid "855de6ac-f3ab-5860-8e8c-3cabd1676a9a" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321139 1074 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible number of participants in the trend. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/d8/d8133f1c4203903bac606bbae61c73717e2ba2dc15a97a77f48eaaeaf4736d06.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =1250y-5{y}^{2} \\\\  & =5y(250-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=250.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $250,$ and we conclude that the maximum possible number of participants is $250.$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321140 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320516 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321140 1069 "q-35348" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321140 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321140 1071 "Given $Z=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 12 & 3 & -6 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 & -7 & 11 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 4 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & 7 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 8 & 2 & -4 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,$ what is $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(Z))?$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321140 1072 #uuid "a52db54f-ed58-568b-ad92-d2a1074b04ad" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321140 1074 "To find $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(Z))$, we reduce $Z$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Z & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 12 & 3 & -6 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 & -7 & 11 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 4 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & 7 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 8 & 2 & -4 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 12 & 3 & -6 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 & -7 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & 7 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 8 & 2 & -4 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{5} & :={R}_{5}+(-\\frac{2}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 12 & 3 & -6 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 & -7 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & 7 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 12 & 3 & -6 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 & -7 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix has $4$ pivot columns and $1$ non-pivot column (namely, the $4$th column). \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(Z))=1.\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321141 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320185 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321141 1069 "q-353483" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321141 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321141 1071 "The download count $P(t)$ of a viral mobile game follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=4P(8\\,000-P),\\,P(0)=600.\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, measured in years. For which value of $P$ is the download count growing at the fastest possible rate?\n\n$P=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321141 1072 #uuid "542b4bd1-a5e6-5564-a6fe-12f10ea4329d" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321141 1074 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFor a logistic growth model, the fastest rate of growth occurs when $P=\\frac{1}{2}a.$\n\n![](images/90/9019b26b417c757bbfab79afb39379c1fc7a892bc44e934c6f23c339deacfd68.png)\n\nFrom our equation, we see that $a=8000.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $8000,$ and $P$ grows at its fastest possible rate when \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{1}{2}a=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8000=4000.\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321142 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320291 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321142 1069 "q-353487" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321142 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321142 1071 "The number $P(t)$ of potential buyers in a market for a new product follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=7200P-3{P}^{2},\\,P(0)=100,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, measured in years. For which value of $P$ is the number of potential buyers growing at the fastest possible rate?\n\n$P=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321142 1072 #uuid "364dd7bd-d9ee-57c0-bf24-91d6f67e88d8" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321142 1074 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFor a logistic growth model, the fastest rate of growth occurs when $P=\\frac{1}{2}a.$\n\n![](images/90/9019b26b417c757bbfab79afb39379c1fc7a892bc44e934c6f23c339deacfd68.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{dt} & =7200P-3{P}^{2} \\\\  & =3P(2400-P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=2400.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $2400,$ and $P$ grows at its fastest possible rate when \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{1}{2}a=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2400=1200.\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321143 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320431 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321143 1069 "q-353490" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321143 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321143 1071 "The number $P(t)$ of stores in a retail chain follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=6300P-7{P}^{2},\\,P(0)=50,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, measured in years. For which value of $P$ is the number of stores growing at the fastest possible rate?\n\n$P=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321143 1072 #uuid "9be06b81-73dc-5b53-bc23-6873f937751f" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321143 1074 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFor a logistic growth model, the fastest rate of growth occurs when $P=\\frac{1}{2}a.$\n\n![](images/90/9019b26b417c757bbfab79afb39379c1fc7a892bc44e934c6f23c339deacfd68.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{dt} & =6300P-7{P}^{2} \\\\  & =7P(900-P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=900.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $900,$ and $P$ grows at its fastest possible rate when \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{1}{2}a=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 900=450.\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321144 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320395 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321144 1069 "q-353520" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321144 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321144 1071 "The half-life of cobalt-60 is $5.27$ years. How long does it take for a $8.0\\,\\mu \\text{g}$ sample of cobalt-60 to reduce to $0.5\\,\\mu \\text{g}?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{yr}$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321144 1072 #uuid "d92e703c-8485-513b-8983-fd7fd22219c5" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321144 1074 "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nThere are two methods that we can use to solve this problem. We describe each of them below.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=5.27$ years, we have\n\n$$\n5.27=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{5.27}\n$$\n\nAlso, since ${M}_{0}=8.0\\,\\mu \\text{g},$ we have the particular solution\n\n$$\nM(t)=8.0{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5.27}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.5\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.5 & =8.0{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5.27}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.5 & =8.0{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5.27} \\\\ \\frac{0.5}{8.0} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5.27} \\\\ \\frac{1}{16} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5.27} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{16}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5.27}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡16 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{5.27} \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡16 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{5.27} \\\\ t & =\\frac{5.27\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡16}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & =\\frac{5.27\\cdot 4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & =21.08\\,\\text{yr}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.5\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{M(t)}{M(0)} & =\\frac{0.5\\,\\mu \\text{g}}{8.0\\,\\mu \\text{g}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\nM(t)={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4}\\cdot M(0).\n$$\n\nFrom this equation, we see that $4$ half-lives must have occurred for the mass to reduce from $8.0\\,\\mu \\text{g}$ to $0.5\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\nt=4\\cdot {t}_{1/2}=4\\cdot 5.27=21.08\\,\\text{yr}.\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321145 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320284 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321145 1069 "q-353523" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321145 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321145 1071 "The half-life of fluorine-18 is $109.7$ minutes. How long does it take for a $6.4\\,\\text{ng}$ sample of fluorine-18 to reduce to $0.2\\,\\text{ng}?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{min}$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321145 1072 #uuid "2c2b0789-a049-58b0-a4c0-cb60d52adde7" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321145 1074 "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nThere are two methods that we can use to solve this problem. We describe each of them below.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=109.7$ minutes, we have\n\n$$\n109.7=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{109.7}\n$$\n\nAlso, since ${M}_{0}=6.4\\,\\text{ng},$ we have the particular solution\n\n$$\nM(t)=6.4{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/109.7}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.2\\,\\text{ng}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.2 & =6.4{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/109.7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.2 & =6.4{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/109.7} \\\\ \\frac{0.2}{6.4} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/109.7} \\\\ \\frac{1}{32} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/109.7} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{32}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/109.7}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡32 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{109.7} \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡32 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{109.7} \\\\ t & =\\frac{109.7\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡32}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & =\\frac{109.7\\cdot 5\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & =548.5\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.2\\,\\text{ng}.$ Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{M(t)}{M(0)} & =\\frac{0.2\\,\\text{ng}}{6.4\\,\\text{ng}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\nM(t)={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5}\\cdot M(0).\n$$\n\nFrom this equation, we see that $5$ half-lives must have occurred for the mass to reduce from $6.4\\,\\text{ng}$ to $0.2\\,\\text{ng}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\nt=5\\cdot {t}_{1/2}=5\\cdot 109.7=548.5\\,\\text{min}.\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321146 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320329 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321146 1069 "q-353546" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321146 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321146 1071 "The half-life of polonium-210 is $138.4$ days. How long does it take for a $8.8\\,\\mu \\text{g}$ sample of polonium-210 to reduce to $1.1\\,\\mu \\text{g}?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{days}$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321146 1072 #uuid "d67921e3-14ad-5197-b390-1f8f4c638645" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321146 1074 "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nThere are two methods that we can use to solve this problem. We describe each of them below.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=138.4$ days, we have\n\n$$\n138.4=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{138.4}\n$$\n\nAlso, since ${M}_{0}=8.8\\,\\mu \\text{g},$ we have the particular solution\n\n$$\nM(t)=8.8{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/138.4}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=1.1\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1.1 & =8.8{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/138.4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1.1 & =8.8{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/138.4} \\\\ \\frac{1.1}{8.8} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/138.4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{8} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/138.4} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{8}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/138.4}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡8 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{138.4} \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡8 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{138.4} \\\\ t & =\\frac{138.4\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡8}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & =\\frac{138.4\\cdot 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & =415.2\\,\\text{days}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=1.1\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{M(t)}{M(0)} & =\\frac{1.1\\,\\mu \\text{g}}{8.8\\,\\mu \\text{g}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\nM(t)={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}\\cdot M(0).\n$$\n\nFrom this equation, we see that $3$ half-lives must have occurred for the mass to reduce from $8.8\\,\\mu \\text{g}$ to $1.1\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\nt=3\\cdot {t}_{1/2}=3\\cdot 138.4=415.2\\,\\text{days}.\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321147 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320247 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321147 1069 "q-353598" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321147 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321147 1071 "![](images/16/16b676776dc22aece5a1fa777007a03b9e7903706cbdf5cc4ae14d8508370419.png)\n\nFind the arc length $L$ of the polar curve $r={e}^{2\\theta }$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above). Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321147 1072 #uuid "e21eed1a-d586-5617-ae1f-53bbb8801576" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321147 1074 "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={e}^{2\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=2{e}^{2\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{({e}^{2\\theta })}^{2}+{(2{e}^{2\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{e}^{4\\theta }+4{e}^{4\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{5{e}^{4\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{[\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2\\theta }]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}{[{e}^{2\\theta }]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}({e}^{2(\\pi /2)}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}({e}^{\\pi }-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321148 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320415 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321148 1069 "q-3536" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321148 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321148 1071 "$\\frac{1}{t}-\\frac{1}{t-2}=$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321148 1072 #uuid "498466a9-3ad8-5bec-927a-f2d8105b2157" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321148 1074 "In this case, we need to put the fractions over a common denominator of $t(t-2),$ since there are no common factors in either denominator that we can exploit.\n\nTo do this, we multiply the top and bottom of the first fraction by $t-2$, and the top and bottom of the second fraction by $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{t}-\\frac{1}{t-2} & = \\\\ \\frac{t-2}{t-2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}-\\frac{t}{t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{t-2} & = \\\\ \\frac{t-2}{(t-2)t}-\\frac{t}{t(t-2)} & = \\\\ \\frac{t-2-t}{(t-2)t} & = \\\\ -\\frac{2}{(t-2)t} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321149 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320627 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321149 1069 "q-353605" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321149 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321149 1071 "Find the arc length $L$ of the polar curve $r={3}^{\\theta /4}$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le 4.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}$\\sqrt{{ln}^{2}⁡3+16}$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321149 1072 #uuid "4c077a15-44c0-5080-9f91-c561226617cf" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321149 1074 "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={3}^{\\theta /4},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }={3}^{\\theta /4}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\sqrt{{({3}^{\\theta /4})}^{2}+{({3}^{\\theta /4}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{4})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\sqrt{{3}^{\\theta /2}+{3}^{\\theta /2}\\cdot \\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡3}{16}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\sqrt{(1+\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡3}{16}){3}^{\\theta /2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡3}{16}}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{3}^{\\theta /4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡3}{16}}\\cdot \\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot {3}^{\\theta /4}\\,{|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡3}{16}}\\cdot \\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot ({3}^{1}-{3}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡3}{16}}\\cdot \\frac{8}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\sqrt{{ln}^{2}⁡3+16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321150 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320296 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321150 1069 "q-353610" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321150 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321150 1071 "In a medical imaging facility, a tracer is found with $25%$ of its original sodium-22 content. Given that the half-life of sodium-22 is $2.6$ years, how old is the tracer? Round your answer to **one decimal place**.\n\nThe tracer is approximately\n{{field-1}}\nyears old." 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321150 1072 #uuid "f6f4f6ad-6fb0-5c26-b7f6-1c819ea19862" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321150 1074 "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=2.6$ years, we have\n\n$$\n2.6=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2.6}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/2.6}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.25{M}_{0}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n0.25{M}_{0}={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/2.6}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.25{M}_{0} & ={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/2.6} \\\\ 0.25\\cancel{{M}_{0}} & =\\cancel{{M}_{0}}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/2.6} \\\\ 0.25 & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/2.6} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.25) & =ln\\,({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/2.6}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.25) & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2.6} \\\\ t & =-\\frac{2.6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.25)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & ≈5.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the tracer is approximately $5.2$ years old." 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321151 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320354 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321151 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320414 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321151 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320629 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321151 1069 "q-353748" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321151 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321151 1071 "Find the first three non-zero terms in the Taylor expansion of $\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}$ about the point $x=1.$\n\n$\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}=${{field-1}}\n$-\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$(x-1)\\,+\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n$(x-1)^{2}-⋯$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321151 1072 #uuid "dda93e8c-172c-5539-b2ea-7f307359ae85" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321151 1074 "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}$ and $a=1.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}={x}^{-4} \\\\ f(1) & ={1}^{-4}=1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{4}{{x}^{5}}=-4{x}^{-5} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =-4(1)^{-5}=-4 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{20}{{x}^{6}}=20{x}^{-6} \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =20(1)^{-6}=20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}} & =1+(-4)(x-1)+\\frac{20}{2!}(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{(-120)}{3!}(x-1)^{3}+⋯ \\\\  & =1-4(x-1)+10(x-1)^{2}-⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321152 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320603 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321152 1069 "q-3538" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321152 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321152 1071 "Given that $\\frac{2}{3p}=a$ and $\\frac{3x}{p}=b,$ what is $a-b?$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321152 1072 #uuid "719a6a52-4256-5448-8450-5f048d104ae7" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321152 1074 "We wish to subtract the following fractions:\n\n$$\na-b=\\frac{2}{3p}-\\frac{3x}{p}\n$$\n\nIn order to subtract the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. Here, we can put both fractions over a common denominator of $3p$ if we multiply the top and bottom of the second fraction by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3p}-\\frac{3x}{p} & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{3p}-\\frac{3}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3x}{p} & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{3p}-\\frac{9x}{3p} & = \\\\ \\frac{2-9x}{3p} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321153 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320426 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321153 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320525 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321153 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320652 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321153 1069 "q-353859" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321153 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321153 1071 "Find the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $x{e}^{x+2}$ about $x=-1.$\n\n$x{e}^{x+2}=$ {{field-1}} $+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$(x+1)^{2}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n$(x+1)^{3}+⋯$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321153 1072 #uuid "6a83ba13-061a-520c-b025-be7998314561" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true] [17592186321153 1074 "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=x{e}^{x+2}$ and $a=-1.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =x{e}^{x+2} \\\\ f(-1) & =(-1){e}^{1}=-e \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x+2}+x{e}^{x+2}=(1+x){e}^{x+2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =(1-1){e}^{1}=0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x+2}+(1+x){e}^{x+2}=(2+x){e}^{x+2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(-1) & =(2-1){e}^{1}=e \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & ={e}^{x+2}+(2+x){e}^{x+2}=(3+x){e}^{x+2} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(-1) & =(3-1){e}^{1}=2e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{x+2} & =-e+(0)(x+1)+\\frac{e}{2!}(x+1)^{2}+\\frac{2e}{3!}(x+1)^{3}⋯ \\\\  & =-e+\\frac{e}{2}(x+1)^{2}+\\frac{e}{3}(x+1)^{3}⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045585 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-003b019d-df96-5f96-9018-d9f41bdd16ea" 17592186319397 "answer-0061ab6a-564a-53b9-92be-37929e8e1d83" 17592186319398 "answer-0077e2b0-9780-54fe-afa4-b670dc9fed69" 17592186319399 "answer-00939ade-063c-559f-bd8d-433b0e5ab9e4" 17592186319400 "answer-00b766a7-d619-5e27-b6a1-be402dc6ec48" 17592186319401 "answer-00b8c2c7-792b-50dd-8748